/**
 * dbml-sharepoint PROBE: LIST VIEW THRESHOLD FIXTURE
 *
 * TWO QUESTIONS, both recorded as unknown in analysis/checks/_views.py:
 *
 *   1. Can a null test be served past the list view threshold when the column
 *      IS indexed? Microsoft's threshold guidance is written for comparison
 *      filters and says nothing about presence tests. Four views in the
 *      shipped template library filter on nothing but is_null (the library's
 *      "blank means still open" idiom), so the answer decides whether they can
 *      be fixed at all or only accepted.
 *
 *   2. Do Created, Modified, Author and Editor behave as indexed? The check
 *      excludes all five system columns, and that exclusion rests on the DBML
 *      side, so it is correct either way, but the project cannot currently
 *      say what SharePoint does.
 *
 * A third rides along: does indexing a PERSON column avert a breach? Microsoft
 * says no. The index article lists "Person or Group (single value) (Lookup)"
 * and the Field element's `Indexed` note says an indexed Lookup column does not
 * prevent exceeding the threshold, and _LOOKUP_FIELD_TYPES already acts on
 * that, but on documentation alone.
 *
 * THE GUARD ROWS, added 2026-08-17 for #272. Every view this tool emits now
 * carries a conjunct that makes the filter editor refuse to open it, so an
 * operator cannot silently truncate a long filter by pressing Save:
 *
 *     <And>{the declared filter}<Or><IsNotNull ID/><IsNull ID/></Or></And>
 *
 * That the conjunct removes no ROWS is measured (caml-chain-depth-probe.js
 * T3, 41 of 41; view-edit-page-probe.js S2). Whether it changes what
 * SharePoint will SERVE past the list view threshold is a different
 * question, and both of those fixtures are far below it: 41 rows and 3.
 *
 * It matters because the conjunct is on ID, which is not the declared
 * filter's column. If adding it changes which predicate the query is
 * planned against, a view that worked at 6,000 rows begins returning a
 * partial answer or none, on the largest lists, and the build cannot see
 * it. `ID` being indexed by default is the reason to expect no change; it
 * is a reason rather than a measurement, which is what these rows fix.
 *
 * GRDIDX and GRDNUL are the measurement. Each sends the filter twice,
 * unguarded then guarded, seconds apart on the same list, and compares the
 * two. That is why they sit OUTSIDE the checkpoint gate the rest of this file
 * uses: they need the list to be past the threshold and nothing more. An
 * absolute expected count would have made them unreadable on 2026-08-17,
 * when the fixture held 5,614 rows of the 6,000 that were loaded; a
 * comparison is readable, because both halves see whatever the list holds. GRDUNI and GRDONLY
 * are the controls that stop a green pair being read as good news. GRDUNI
 * guards the UNINDEXED twin, which CMPUNI establishes is refused at this
 * size; if guarding it makes it SERVED, the conjunct is changing plan
 * selection and the whole comparison is suspect. GRDONLY sends the guard
 * with no filter at all, matching every row in the list, and must be
 * refused for the same reason.
 *
 * WHY A NEW FIXTURE: native-index-probe.js ran on 2026-07-30 and established
 * none of it. Its metadata half is void (SP.Field.Indexed read false for ID
 * itself), and its behavioural half never ran, because the site's largest
 * generic list held 21 items against a threshold of 5,000. The blocker was the
 * fixture, not the method.
 *
 * THIS PROBE WRITES. Two lists, six columns, four indexes, four views, and the
 * operator then loads 6,000 rows into it. Both write gates must be turned on
 * deliberately; a pasted, unedited copy prints its plan and stops.
 *
 * MATCHED SELECTIVITY IS THE DESIGN. Five filters match exactly one row in a
 * hundred, on disjoint row sets, so each differs from the positive control in
 * exactly ONE respect (the index, the operator, or the field type). The four
 * system-column filters are NOT matched and cannot be compared with them; each
 * says so in its own evidence, because a comment nobody copies out of the
 * console is not a safeguard.
 *
 * WHAT IT ASKS
 *   RUNCNT  live ItemCount matches a declared checkpoint
 *   IDXSET  the four intended indexes really are set, and Shadow is not
 *           (SortBait is excluded because it is EXPECTED to turn over; see COLUMNS)
 *   FLAGS   Indexed/AutoIndexed for all six columns, every run
 *   CMPIDX  OData comparison, INDEXED Text        (positive control)
 *   CMPUNI  OData comparison, UNINDEXED twin      (negative control)
 *   NULIDX  OData null test, INDEXED DateTime
 *   CMPCAM  CAML  comparison, INDEXED Text
 *   GRDIDX  CAML  comparison, INDEXED Text, GUARDED   (#272)
 *   GRDNUL  CAML  <IsNull>, INDEXED DateTime, GUARDED (#272)
 *   GRDUNI  CAML  comparison, UNINDEXED twin, GUARDED (#272 negative control)
 *   GRDONLY CAML  the guard ALONE, matching every row (#272 negative control)
 *   NULCAM  CAML  <IsNull>, INDEXED DateTime      (the question as _views.py asks it)
 *   PERSID  OData comparison, INDEXED Person
 *   LOOKID  OData comparison, INDEXED Lookup
 *   PERCAM  CAML  Eq, INDEXED Person, LookupId form
 *   PERCAV  CAML  Eq, INDEXED Person, projected-value form
 *   LOOCAM  CAML  Eq, INDEXED Lookup, LookupId form
 *   NNIDX   CAML  <IsNotNull>, INDEXED DateTime
 *   NNUNI   CAML  <IsNotNull>, UNINDEXED DateTime  (is an index NECESSARY?)
 *   LOOPRJ  CAML  Eq, INDEXED Lookup, PROJECTING it   (the real view shape)
 *   PERPRJ  CAML  Eq, INDEXED Person, PROJECTING it   (the real view shape)
 *   JOINMAX how many Lookup columns can one view PROJECT?   (issue #44)
 *   JOINSYS does Created By count as a join?
 *   JOINEDT does Modified By count as a join?
 *   JOINPER does a Person column count as a join?
 *   JOINPRJ does a lookup's additional-field projection cost its own join?
 *   ANDIDX  CAML  And, INDEXED condition FIRST     (does filter ORDER decide?)
 *   ANDUNI  CAML  And, UNINDEXED condition FIRST   (same 60 rows, reversed)
 *   CALCAM  CAML  Eq on a CALCULATED column   (OData refuses this by type)
 *   SEEDCK  the seeded rows really hold both values, read via an INDEXED filter
 *   TGTIDX  is the lookup target's ShowField indexed on this run?
 *   TGTENU  enumerate the lookup target, unordered   (what a picker opens with)
 *   TGTORD  enumerate it ordered by ShowField
 *   TGTPFX  type-ahead against it
 *   LKPCAL  can a Lookup show a CALCULATED column from its target?
 *   TGTCAL  order the target by that calculated label
 *   TGTSFO  INDEXED filter first, THEN order  (the variant that can work)
 *   PIKIDX  picker choices on an INDEXED ShowField   (the form's own call)
 *   PIKCAL  picker choices on a CALCULATED ShowField
 *   PIKCND  picker choices on the status-driven label
 *   PIKVW   does the picker honour the target's DEFAULT VIEW filter?
 *   TGTSTA  the INDEXED status filter on the target
 *   TGTCND  order by the STATUS-DRIVEN conditional label
 *   TGTFLT  type-ahead against that conditional label
 *   TGTSTO  INDEXED status filter first, THEN order by Title
 *   CALCIDX can a Calculated column carry an index?
 *   NOTEIDX can a Multi-line Text column carry an index?
 *   SYSCRE  Created     SYSMOD  Modified     SYSAUT  Author     SYSEDI  Editor
 *
 * BOTH OData AND CAML, deliberately. analysis/checks/_views.py asks about a
 * CAML <IsNull>, because that is what a SharePoint VIEW renders. OData $filter
 * is a different code path, and `eq null` is not in Microsoft's documented
 * operator list for the REST service at all. Measuring only OData would leave
 * the recorded unknown untouched while the summary read "all answered".
 *
 * HOW TO RUN: the run plan, in order
 *   1. Open a SharePoint site you are willing to put a 6,000-row list on.
 *   2. Set CONFIRMED and ALLOW_WRITES true. Paste. This provisions everything
 *      and prints the two ids the row generator needs.
 *   3. Locally:  .venv/Scripts/python.exe test/manual/make_threshold_rows.py \
 *                    --owner-id <printed> --parent-id <printed>
 *   4. Set OWNER_ID below to the same value you passed --owner-id.
 *   5. Load threshold-rows-01-to-1000.csv. Re-paste. Snapshot at 1,000.
 *   6. Load 02. Re-paste with RUN_LABEL='before sort'. Then OPEN the
 *      'Sort bait' view in the browser. Then re-paste with
 *      RUN_LABEL='after sort'. That pair is the only thing that can establish
 *      what AutoIndexed means.
 *   7. Load 03, 04, 05, re-pasting after each. Copy every RESULTS block.
 *
 * Re-pasting is safe at any point: provisioning is idempotent and later runs
 * only measure.
 *
 * NOTE: the harness's CLEANUP flag does NOTHING here. This probe never calls
 * resetList(), on purpose. You do not want a 6,000-row fixture silently
 * emptied because a flag was left on. Teardown is CLEANUP_AT_END, at the
 * bottom, and it is a separate deliberate act.
 *
 * STATUS: run 2026-07-31 on one site, at 1,000 / 3,000 / 6,000 items.
 *
 *   CONTROLS VALID AT 6,000, which is what makes the rest readable. The
 *   positive control (an indexed Text comparison matching 60 of 6,000) was
 *   SERVED. The negative control (the same comparison on Shadow, byte-
 *   identical data, no index) was REFUSED, HTTP 500
 *   SPQueryThrottledException.
 *
 *   THAT LAST SENTENCE USED TO READ "Shadow was never auto-indexed at any
 *   size", and the run of 2026-08-17 falsified it: Shadow came back
 *   Indexed=true, AutoIndexed=true, so SharePoint had indexed it on its
 *   own between runs. The negative control expires when that happens, and
 *   with it every indexed-versus-unindexed comparison in this file. IDXCLR
 *   now tries to clear the flag before anything is measured and says
 *   whether the clear stuck. Treat "not auto-indexed" as a per-run
 *   observation rather than a property of the column.
 *
 *   ANSWERED: a CAML <IsNull> on an INDEXED column IS served past the
 *   threshold: 60 rows, exact, HTTP 200. So is the OData `eq null` form, which
 *   Microsoft's operator list for the REST service does not document at all.
 *
 *   NOT the lookup rule, though it reads like it: filters on an INDEXED
 *   Person and an INDEXED Lookup were served, 60 of 6,000 each, on OData and
 *   CAML and in both filter forms. Every one of those queries FILTERED on the
 *   lookup without PROJECTING it: `$select=Id`, and CAML with no <ViewFields>.
 *   A filter reads the stored id off the row; the rule is about the JOIN, which
 *   a query only pays for when the column is in the view's fields. Adding
 *   `Parent` to an All Items view of this fixture does fail, with the 5,000-item
 *   threshold message. LOOPRJ and PERPRJ are the measurement that settles it.
 *
 *   ANSWERED: what sets AutoIndexed. Opening a modern view sorted on an
 *   unindexed, high-cardinality Text column at 3,000 items made SharePoint
 *   create an index, flipping Indexed AND AutoIndexed false -> true together,
 *   on that column alone. Isolated by the before/after pair at the same row
 *   count. It persisted to 6,000 and did not revert. Two corollaries:
 *   AutoIndexed IS exposed over REST here, and the flags are not independent
 *   (an auto-created index sets both).
 *
 *   ANSWERED: an index is NECESSARY for a presence test, not merely
 *   sufficient, and the failure without one is SILENT. Matched pair, identical
 *   values on the same 60 of 6,000 rows, differing only in the index: the
 *   indexed column returned 60, the unindexed one returned 50 (HTTP 200, no
 *   error). SEEDCK confirmed all 60 rows really hold both values, so it is
 *   truncation, not missing data. Note it truncated to 50, not to the
 *   documented 1,250 fallback; that figure is a ceiling, not a prediction.
 *
 *   ANSWERED: CAML AND OData DISAGREE, and the difference is the whole shape
 *   of a threshold failure. On an unindexed or unindexable column past 5,000:
 *
 *     OData $filter  HTTP 500 SPQueryThrottledException, a loud refusal
 *     CAML           HTTP 200 with a PARTIAL answer and no error at all
 *
 *   The CAML window is the first 5,000 rows in id order: NNIDX spans rows
 *   3..5903 and returns 60, NNUNI spans 3..4903 and returns 50, on identical
 *   data. So a CAML result can be complete, silently short, or accidentally
 *   correct because every match happens to sit inside the window, which is
 *   what CALCAM is: one match, at row 3, on a CALCULATED column that OData
 *   refuses by type ("cannot be used in the query filter expression") and CAML
 *   serves. A view renders CAML, so CAML is what governs _views.py.
 *
 *   ANSWERED: THERE IS NO NATIVE FILTERED LOOKUP. Every native lever is now
 *   measured and all three are closed:
 *
 *     the Field element        no attribute restricts a Lookup's choices; it
 *                              carries List, ShowField, Mult and nothing else
 *     a conditional ShowField  PIKCAL / PIKCND, REFUSED past 5,000. A
 *                              Calculated column cannot be indexed, so the
 *                              picker's enumeration has to scan
 *     the target's default     PIKVW, IGNORED. With 'Active only' filtering
 *     view                     PickStatus and DefaultView reading true, the
 *                              picker still returned LookupId 2001 where the
 *                              Active rows are 1..1000
 *
 *   The middle one is the workaround the community converged on, so its
 *   ceiling matters: it works on a small target and fails on the large one
 *   that motivates asking.
 *
 *   What DOES work is PIKIDX: an INDEXED, indexable ShowField is served where
 *   both others are refused. So a Lookup into a list that may pass 5,000 needs
 *   its target's ShowField indexed and not Calculated, or the new-item form
 *   cannot be used at all. Restricting WHICH rows are offered needs something
 *   outside the list schema (an SPFx form control or a curated target list).
 *
 *   ANSWERED: the conditional-label scheme cannot work. A Lookup whose
 *   ShowField is a Calculated column is ACCEPTED at provisioning, and then that
 *   column can be used in neither a sort nor a filter expression over OData,
 *   by TYPE, at any list size, with real values present in the status column
 *   driving it. What does work is TGTSTO: an INDEXED filter first, then order.
 *
 *   NOT ESTABLISHED: Created, Modified, Author and Editor. All four were
 *   refused at 6,000, which this fixture cannot read as evidence: the loader
 *   owns every row, so each matches the whole list and breaches on result-set
 *   size. Needs rows from several principals over several intervals.
 *
 *   ANSWERED: the lookup target's size does not change the view result. With
 *   6,500 items in the TARGET and 6,000 in the source, the projecting query is
 *   still served, 60 of 60. The target size is printed in the question, so runs
 *   at different sizes are distinguishable in the RESULTS block itself.
 *
 *   OPEN: the lookup PICKER. Past 5,000 in the target, the new-item form
 *   refuses: "This is a lookup column that displays data from another list that
 *   currently exceeds the List View Threshold defined by the administrator
 *   (5000)." A view reads the column; nobody can set it. TGTENU / TGTORD /
 *   TGTPFX ask the query shapes a picker needs; run them once with
 *   INDEX_SHOWFIELD off and once on, since one run says what those queries do
 *   and not what an index does to them.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REVISION ADDS, both aimed at the two answers above that were
 * measured on the wrong path or without a control:
 *
 *   PERCAM / PERCAV / LOOCAM put the Person and Lookup question in CAML, which
 *   is what a VIEW renders and what _views.py governs, in both the stored-id
 *   and projected-value forms. If these agree with the OData result, the
 *   documented lookup rule is contradicted on the path that matters.
 *
 *   NNIDX / NNUNI answer whether an index is NECESSARY for a presence test or
 *   only sufficient. Two DateTime columns, identical data on the same 60 rows,
 *   differing only in the index, tested with <IsNotNull>. The probe seeds them
 *   itself (60 updates by id, which do not go through the threshold).
 *
 *   Inverted deliberately: a column added to a list that already holds its rows
 *   is blank on ALL of them, so an IsNull over it matches everything and
 *   breaches on result-set size, which is the problem the SYS* rows have.
 *   Asking IsNotNull over a seeded 60 makes the small side the matched side.
 *
 *   NOTE: OWNER_ID resets to 0 on every re-render, and the four Person
 *   questions are not sent at all when it is 0. Set it before pasting.
 */
(async () => {
  // ======================================================================
  // ==  EVERYTHING YOU EDIT IS HERE: this block, plus CONFIRMED /        ==
  // ==  ALLOW_WRITES / CLEANUP in the three lines directly beneath it.    ==
  // ==  Those three are the shared harness's, which is why they sit just  ==
  // ==  the other side of the include rather than in here. Nothing        ==
  // ==  further down the file needs touching, ever.                       ==
  // ======================================================================

  // Names this run in every evidence row. The probe CANNOT tell one run from
  // another (a fresh JavaScript context has no memory and nowhere to keep one),
  // so two pastes at the same row count are told apart by this and nothing
  // else. Set it EVERY paste, and add a reason on any repeat.
  const RUN_LABEL = '';

  // Site user id for the Person questions. Left at 0 the probe discovers it
  // from the fixture's own rows, which is the value the data carries by
  // definition. Set it to pin one deliberately.
  //
  // NOT read from web/currentuser: a paste from a different account would
  // retarget the filter at an id no row carries, match nothing, return HTTP 200
  // and read as SERVED.
  const OWNER_ID = 0;

  // --- Write gates, all shipping off ------------------------------------
  // Each is a separate act because each writes something different.

  // Recycles both fixture lists. Expect more than one pass past 5,000 rows.
  const CLEANUP_AT_END = false;

  // Index the lookup target's ShowField (its Title). TGTENU / TGTORD / TGTPFX
  // are a matched pair ACROSS TWO RUNS, once with this off and once on: one run
  // says what the picker's queries do, not what the index does to them.
  const INDEX_SHOWFIELD = false;

  // Mark the first TARGET_ACTIVE rows of the target 'Active', so the
  // status-driven conditional label has something to be conditional ABOUT.
  // Make the target's 'Active only' view its DEFAULT, then RE-PASTE. PIKIDX is
  // then a matched pair across two runs, and the highest LookupId it returns
  // says whether a native filtered lookup exists at all.
  const FILTER_TARGET_VIEW = false;

  const SEED_TARGET_STATUS = false;
  const TARGET_ACTIVE = 1000;

  // Grow the lookup TARGET to PARENT_TARGET, so the projecting query joins into
  // a list that is itself past the threshold. Nothing else moves: every child
  // row still points at the same parent item, so the filter matches the same 60
  // rows. The one genuinely large write here, hence its own gate.
  const LOAD_PARENT = false;
  const PARENT_TARGET = 6000;

  // Operations per $batch. SharePoint documents $batch but not a per-request
  // ceiling, so this is chosen conservatively rather than read off a limit.
  const BATCH_SIZE = 100;

  // --- Build the fixture from here, with no CSV and no flow -------------
  // 0 loads nothing. 1..5 load the list up to CHECKPOINTS[TEST_PHASE - 1]
  // (1,000 / 3,000 / 4,900 / 5,100 / 6,000), creating only the rows that are
  // missing, so raising the number continues rather than restarts.
  //
  // Phase 2 is the one that needs a human between pastes: take it, open the
  // 'Sort bait' view, then re-paste. That before/after pair at the same row
  // count is the only thing that can say what AutoIndexed means.
  //
  // Rows come from _threshold_rows.js.j2, which composes exactly what
  // make_threshold_rows.py writes. A node-executed test compares the two field
  // by field over all 6,000 rows, so the CSV path stays available and neither
  // can drift from the other unnoticed.
  const TEST_PHASE = 0;

  // --- The list view LOOKUP threshold (issue #44) -----------------------
  // Creates JOIN_COLUMNS empty Lookup columns on the fixture so JOINMAX can
  // walk up and find where a view stops rendering. 14 to bracket both the
  // documented 8 and the documented 12 with headroom either side. Ships off:
  // it adds real columns, and a fixture only needs them once.
  const PROBE_JOINS = false;
  const JOIN_COLUMNS = 14;

  // ======================================================================
  // ==  END OF THIS PROBE'S SETTINGS, the harness's three gates are      ==
  // ==  immediately below, and that is the last of it.                    ==
  // ======================================================================

{% include "_probe_harness.js.j2" %}

  // Printed FIRST, before any gate, so it appears even on a run that stops
  // immediately. A stale clipboard and a fix that did not work produce
  // identical transcripts otherwise. This has already cost a round trip of
  // diagnosis, where the only tell was a stack-trace line number. Injected by
  // render_probes.py from a hash of this template and every partial.
  log('INFO', 'probe revision {{ revision }}. Quote this when reporting results.');

  // Say it at RUN TIME, not only in the header. An operator set this flag,
  // reasonably believed it was resetting the fixture between runs, and read
  // four accumulated parent rows as "a new list each time", when in fact the
  // same two lists had persisted throughout. A flag that is on and silently
  // does nothing is worse than one that does not exist, and the header comment
  // saying so was not enough.
  if (CLEANUP) {
    log('FAIL', 'CLEANUP is ON and is IGNORED by this probe. It resets nothing.');
    log('FAIL', 'resetList() is never called here, deliberately: you do not want');
    log('FAIL', 'a 6,000-row fixture silently emptied because a flag was left on.');
    log('FAIL', 'Teardown is CLEANUP_AT_END, at the bottom. Your lists persist.');
  }

  const LIST = 'dbmlsp Probe Threshold';
  const PARENT = 'dbmlsp Probe Threshold Parent';
  const CHECKPOINTS = [1000, 3000, 4900, 5100, 6000];
  const LIST_VIEW_THRESHOLD = 5000;
  const RARE_BUCKET = 'Z';
  // The generator's TOTAL and MATCHING_ROWS, and the ratio derived from them
  // rather than written out separately, so a constant that drifts from the
  // generator changes the arithmetic instead of sitting in a comment being
  // wrong. test_probes.py pins both against make_threshold_rows.py.
  const FIXTURE_TOTAL = 6000;
  const MATCHING_ROWS = 60;
  const PER_HUNDRED = FIXTURE_TOTAL / MATCHING_ROWS;
  // Enough headroom to COUNT the matches rather than just prove one exists.
  const PAGE = 200;

  expect('RUNCNT', 'Live ItemCount matches a declared checkpoint');
  expect('IDXSET', 'The intended indexes are set and the controls are not');
  expect('IDXCLR', 'Can the negative control be un-indexed again?');
  expect('FLAGS', 'Indexed/AutoIndexed for all six columns');
  expect('CMPIDX', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Text (positive control)');
  expect('CMPUNI', 'OData comparison, UNINDEXED twin of Bucket (negative control)');
  expect('NULIDX', 'OData null test, INDEXED DateTime');
  expect('CMPCAM', 'CAML comparison, INDEXED Text');
  expect('GRDIDX', 'CAML comparison, INDEXED Text, GUARDED as #272 emits it');
  expect('GRDNUL', 'CAML IsNull, INDEXED DateTime, GUARDED as #272 emits it');
  expect('GRDUNI', 'CAML comparison, UNINDEXED twin, GUARDED (control)');
  expect('GRDONLY', 'CAML the guard ALONE, matching every row (control)');
  expect('NULCAM', 'CAML IsNull, INDEXED DateTime (the question as _views.py asks it)');
  expect('PERSID', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Person');
  expect('LOOKID', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Lookup');
  expect('SYSCRE', 'OData comparison on Created');
  expect('SYSMOD', 'OData comparison on Modified');
  expect('SYSAUT', 'OData comparison on Author');
  expect('SYSEDI', 'OData comparison on Editor');
  expect('SEEDCK', 'The seeded rows really do hold both values');
  expect('LKPCAL', 'Can a Lookup show a CALCULATED column from its target?');
  expect('TGTCAL', 'Order the lookup target by the CALCULATED label');
  expect('TGTSFO', 'INDEXED filter first, THEN order by ShowField');
  expect('TGTSTA', 'The INDEXED status filter on the lookup target');
  expect('PIKIDX', 'Picker choices on an INDEXED ShowField');
  expect('PIKCAL', 'Picker choices on a CALCULATED ShowField');
  expect('PIKCND', 'Picker choices on the STATUS-DRIVEN label');
  expect('PIKVW', "Does the picker honour the target's DEFAULT VIEW filter?");
  expect('TGTCND', 'Order the target by the STATUS-DRIVEN conditional label');
  expect('TGTFLT', 'Type-ahead against the STATUS-DRIVEN conditional label');
  expect('TGTSTO', 'INDEXED status filter first, THEN order by Title');
  expect('JOINMAX', 'How many Lookup columns can one view project?');
  expect('JOINSYS', 'Does Created By count as a join?');
  expect('JOINEDT', 'Does Modified By count as a join?');
  expect('JOINPER', 'Does a Person column count as a join?');
  expect('JOINPRJ', "Does a lookup's additional-field projection cost its own join?");
  expect('ANDIDX', 'CAML And, INDEXED condition FIRST');
  expect('ANDUNI', 'CAML And, UNINDEXED condition FIRST (same rows, reversed)');
  expect('CALCAM', 'CAML Eq on a CALCULATED column (OData refuses this by type)');
  expect('TGTIDX', "The lookup target's ShowField index state");
  expect('TGTENU', 'Enumerate the lookup target, unordered');
  expect('TGTORD', 'Enumerate the lookup target ordered by ShowField');
  expect('TGTPFX', 'Type-ahead against the lookup target');
  expect('CALCIDX', 'Can a Calculated column be indexed?');
  expect('NOTEIDX', 'Can a Multi-line Text column be indexed?');
  expect('LOOPRJ', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Lookup, PROJECTING the lookup (the real view shape)');
  expect('PERPRJ', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, PROJECTING the person (the real view shape)');
  expect('PERCAM', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, LookupId form');
  expect('PERCAV', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, projected-value form');
  expect('LOOCAM', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Lookup, LookupId form');
  expect('NNIDX', 'CAML IsNotNull, INDEXED DateTime');
  expect('NNUNI', 'CAML IsNotNull, UNINDEXED DateTime (is an index NECESSARY?)');

  const odata = (name) => encodeURIComponent(String(name).replace(/'/g, "''"));

  // [name, index expectation, schema XML]. Shadow mirrors Bucket's data
  // exactly and is deliberately NOT indexed, so that pair differs only in the
  // index. SortBait is the column a modern-view sort is aimed at.
  //
  // THREE VALUES, NOT TWO:
  //
  //   true   must be indexed  (a filter column a measurement depends on)
  //   false  must NOT be      (the negative control, same)
  //   null   OBSERVED, never asserted
  //
  // AN AUTO-CREATED INDEX SPENDS A REAL SLOT. SortBait appears on the Indexed
  // Columns page as "SortBait (Automatically created)" and consumes one of the
  // twenty. So opening a sorted view on an unindexed column does not merely set
  // a flag. It takes budget a deployer is separately trying to allocate, with
  // nothing in a build or a deploy able to see it happen.
  //
  // COMPOUND INDEXES ARE OUT OF REACH FROM HERE, and that is measured rather
  // than assumed. A HAR of the classic Indexed Columns page creating one shows:
  //
  //   - It is an ASP.NET WebForms postback to _layouts/15/EditIndex.aspx?List=
  //     <guid>, form-urlencoded, carrying primaryColumnDropDown and
  //     secondaryColumnDropDown as column GUIDs. It needs a __VIEWSTATE and
  //     __EVENTVALIDATION pair harvested from a prior GET of that page, so it
  //     cannot be issued statelessly.
  //   - NO _api or client.svc call is involved. Searching the whole capture for
  //     SPFieldIndex, FieldIndexes, AddIndex or GetIndexedColumns finds nothing:
  //     the UI does not use a REST path, so a deployer has none to copy.
  //   - The readback is HTML only, and it does NOT label which column is
  //     primary: an index renders as one comma-joined link, "ColA, ColB", with
  //     order the only clue. Each index does carry its own GUID in the edit
  //     link's Index= parameter.
  //
  // Two consequences for this probe. FLAGS reads a per-field boolean, so a
  // column that is half of a compound index is indistinguishable from one with
  // an index of its own. IDXSET cannot see index SHAPE and does not claim to.
  // And the only place the true count lives, including auto-created ones, is
  // that page's own sentence: "You have created N of maximum 20 indices on this
  // list." Nothing reachable from script reports it.
  //
  // SortBait is null because its job is to CHANGE. It exists to be sorted on
  // so SharePoint auto-indexes it, and the before/after pair of FLAGS rows is
  // the only thing in this probe that can say what AutoIndexed means. No filter
  // here reads SortBait, so its index state cannot affect any measurement.
  //
  // Asserting over it would be a control that voids the whole table the moment
  // the experiment starts working. An auto-index does not revert, so every
  // later paste is poisoned too. A control must cover the values a measurement
  // DEPENDS on, never the values it OBSERVES.
  const COLUMNS = [
    ['Bucket',   true,  '<Field Type="Text" DisplayName="Bucket" Name="Bucket" MaxLength="255"/>'],
    ['Shadow',   false, '<Field Type="Text" DisplayName="Shadow" Name="Shadow" MaxLength="255"/>'],
    ['SortBait', null,  '<Field Type="Text" DisplayName="SortBait" Name="SortBait" MaxLength="255"/>'],
    ['ClosedAt', true,  '<Field Type="DateTime" DisplayName="ClosedAt" Name="ClosedAt" Format="DateTime"/>'],
    ['Owner',    true,  '<Field Type="User" DisplayName="Owner" Name="Owner" UserSelectionMode="PeopleOnly"/>'],
    // The is-NOT-null pair. Identical DateTime columns differing ONLY in the
    // index, populated on the same 60 rows by seedNotNull() below.
    //
    // NOT-null rather than null, and that is the whole trick. A column added to
    // a list that already holds its rows is blank on ALL of them, so an IsNull
    // over it matches every row and breaches on result-set size, saying
    // nothing about the index, exactly like the SYS* rows. Inverting the test
    // makes the small side the matched side: populate 60 and ask IsNotNull.
    // 60 item updates instead of a 5,940-row backfill.
    ['NotNullIdx', true,  '<Field Type="DateTime" DisplayName="NotNullIdx" Name="NotNullIdx" Format="DateTime"/>'],
    ['NotNullUni', false, '<Field Type="DateTime" DisplayName="NotNullUni" Name="NotNullUni" Format="DateTime"/>'],
  ];

  // The value seeded into both is-not-null columns. Any value works; a constant
  // keeps the two columns byte-identical, so the pair differs only in the index.
  const SEED_STAMP = '2020-06-15T00:00:00Z';

  if (!CONFIRMED) {
    log('INFO', `Would create lists '${PARENT}' and '${LIST}', add 6 columns,`);
    log('INFO', 'set Indexed=true on Bucket, ClosedAt, Owner and Parent, create 4');
    log('INFO', 'views, then print the ids needed by make_threshold_rows.py.');
    log('INFO', 'Nothing has been written. Set CONFIRMED and ALLOW_WRITES to true.');
    return;
  }
  if (!ALLOW_WRITES) {
    log('INFO', 'CONFIRMED, but ALLOW_WRITES is false. Stopping without writes.');
    return;
  }

  // ---- Provision: PARENT FIRST ---------------------------------------
  // The child's Lookup field needs the parent list's id in its schema XML.
  // Cleanup reverses this; see the CLEANUP_AT_END block.
  const listShape = '?$select=Id,ItemCount,ListItemEntityTypeFullName';
  const ensureList = async (title, bootId) => {
    const existing = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')${listShape}`);
    if (existing.ok) return existing.body;
    const digest = await getDigest();
    const created = await spPost('web/lists', {
      Title: title,
      BaseTemplate: 100,
      Description: 'dbml-sharepoint list view threshold probe. Safe to recycle.',
    }, digest);
    if (!created.ok) {
      record(bootId, `Create ${title}`, 'FAIL',
             `HTTP ${created.status}: ${created.text.slice(0, 400)}`);
      return null;
    }
    // Re-read: the create response shape varies with OData mode and can be
    // empty, and the child's Lookup schema needs a MEASURED id.
    const reread = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')${listShape}`);
    if (readFailed(reread) || !reread.body.Id) {
      record(bootId, `Read back ${title}`, 'FAIL',
             `HTTP ${reread.status}: no usable list Id`);
      return null;
    }
    return reread.body;
  };

  // Separate boot ids, not one shared 'BOOT': record() overwrites by id, so a
  // single id would let whichever list failed second erase the first, and the
  // surviving row would name the wrong list.
  const parent = await ensureList(PARENT, 'BOOTPARENT');
  const main = await ensureList(LIST, 'BOOTMAIN');
  if (!parent || !main) {
    report();
    return;
  }

  // ---- Columns and indexes -------------------------------------------
  const fieldsPath = `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/fields`;
  const readField = async (name) =>
    // No $select: an unrecognised property in $select errors the WHOLE
    // request, so asking for AutoIndexed on a tenant that does not expose it
    // would report every column unreadable and read as a permissions problem.
    spGet(`${fieldsPath}/getbyinternalnameortitle('${name}')`);

  const addField = async (schemaXml) => {
    const digest = await getDigest();
    return spPost(`${fieldsPath}/createfieldasxml`,
                  { parameters: { SchemaXml: schemaXml, Options: 8 } }, digest);
  };

  // Indexed is read/write and documented as "TRUE if the column is indexed for
  // use in view filters". Set on the EMPTY list: Microsoft's troubleshooting
  // guidance puts a 20,000-item ceiling on adding or removing an indexed
  // column, and provisioning first removes the question entirely.
  //
  // VERBOSE OData for this one call, and that is not optional. The body needs
  // __metadata, and odata=nometadata REJECTS the type hint rather than
  // ignoring it: "The property '__metadata' does not exist on type ...". The
  // first live run of this probe failed all four MERGEs with HTTP 400 for
  // exactly that reason. The body was byte-identical to the deployer's proven
  // patchField, but _http_write.js.j2 sends verbose and the probe harness
  // sends nometadata. calculated-choice-operand.js and datetime-sentinel-probe
  // .js both already carried the lesson in a comment; this is the fourth time
  // this repository has paid for it.
  //
  // Overriding Content-Type in extraHeaders works because the harness spreads
  // extraHeaders LAST, so a caller can replace a default rather than only add.
  const setIndexed = async (name, indexed = true) => {
    const digest = await getDigest();
    return spPost(`${fieldsPath}/getbyinternalnameortitle('${name}')`,
                  { __metadata: { type: 'SP.Field' }, Indexed: indexed }, digest,
                  {
                    Accept: 'application/json;odata=verbose',
                    'Content-Type': 'application/json;odata=verbose',
                    'X-HTTP-Method': 'MERGE',
                    'IF-MATCH': '*',
                  });
  };

  const schemas = COLUMNS.concat([[
    'Parent', true,
    `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="Parent" Name="Parent" ` +
    `List="{${parent.Id}}" ShowField="Title"/>`,
  ]]);

  for (const [name, indexed, schemaXml] of schemas) {
    if (readFailed(await readField(name))) {
      const made = await addField(schemaXml);
      if (!made.ok) {
        log('FAIL', `Could not create ${name}: HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
        continue;
      }
    }
    if (!indexed) continue;
    const flagged = await setIndexed(name);
    log(flagged.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `${name}: Indexed=true ${flagged.ok ? 'requested' : `FAILED HTTP ${flagged.status}`}`);
  }

  // One parent row for every child row to point at, and its REAL id.
  //
  // Asks for the items rather than trusting SP.List.ItemCount, which lags:
  // three consecutive runs all read ItemCount 0 on a list that already had a
  // row and each added another. Harmless to the measurements, but the run plan
  // pastes this seven more times and "idempotent" has to mean it.
  //
  // The id is then MEASURED rather than assumed to be 1. A hardcoded 1 is a
  // filter that silently matches nothing if item 1 is ever recycled. The
  // match-count check would catch it, but as a mystery rather than a cause.
  const parentItems = await spGet(
    `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/items?$select=Id&$top=1&$orderby=Id asc`);
  let parentItemId = (!readFailed(parentItems) && parentItems.body.value
                      && parentItems.body.value.length)
    ? parentItems.body.value[0].Id
    : 0;
  if (!parentItemId) {
    const digest = await getDigest();
    const seeded = await spPost(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/items`, { Title: 'Probe parent' }, digest);
    parentItemId = (seeded.ok && seeded.body && seeded.body.Id) ? seeded.body.Id : 0;
    log(seeded.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `parent item ${seeded.ok ? `created, Id=${parentItemId}` : `FAILED HTTP ${seeded.status}`}`);
  } else {
    log('OK', `parent item already present, Id=${parentItemId}`);
  }

  // ---- One batch writer, used by everything that writes in bulk --------
  // _api/$batch: multipart/mixed carrying one changeset of write operations.
  // Three callers need this (creating fixture rows, growing the lookup target,
  // stamping a status column), and three hand-rolled copies of a MIME envelope
  // is three chances to get a boundary or a verb wrong. It has already been
  // both: CRLF between parts is required by the boundary parser, and the verb
  // belongs in the part's own REQUEST LINE, not in an X-HTTP-Method header. A
  // changeset part is just text, so `MERGE url HTTP/1.1` is what MERGE looks
  // like; sent as POST with the tunnel header, SharePoint reads a POST to a
  // specific item and rejects every operation in the batch.
  //
  // ops: [{ method, url, body, verbose }]. `verbose` where the body carries
  // __metadata, which odata=nometadata rejects outright rather than ignoring.
  let batchSeq = 0;
  const spBatch = async (ops) => {
    batchSeq += 1;
    const batch = `batch_dbmlsp_${batchSeq}`;
    const changeset = `changeset_dbmlsp_${batchSeq}`;
    // CRLF as a named constant rather than an escape inside a dozen string
    // literals. MIME boundaries are parsed on exactly this, so it is load
    // bearing, and it reads as noise when spelled out on every line.
    const NL = '\r\n';
    const parts = ops.map((op) => {
      const mode = op.verbose ? 'verbose' : 'nometadata';
      return `--${changeset}${NL}`
        + `Content-Type: application/http${NL}`
        + `Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary${NL}${NL}`
        + `${op.method} ${op.url} HTTP/1.1${NL}`
        + `Content-Type: application/json;odata=${mode}${NL}`
        + `Accept: application/json;odata=${mode}${NL}`
        + (op.method === 'POST' ? '' : `IF-MATCH: *${NL}`)
        + `${NL}${JSON.stringify(op.body)}${NL}`;
    });
    const res = await fetch(`${WEB}/_api/$batch`, {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': `multipart/mixed; boundary=${batch}`,
        Accept: 'application/json;odata=nometadata',
        'X-RequestDigest': await getDigest(),
      },
      body: `--${batch}${NL}`
            + `Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=${changeset}${NL}${NL}`
            + parts.join('')
            + `--${changeset}--${NL}${NL}--${batch}--${NL}`,
    });
    const text = await res.text();
    // What the batch ACTUALLY did, read from the inner status lines. The
    // envelope answers 200 whether or not the writes inside it worked
    // (SharePoint's batch is explicitly non-transactional), and SP.List
    // .ItemCount lags, so neither can be believed here.
    const codes = (text.match(/^HTTP\/1\.1 (\d{3})/gm) || [])
      .map((line) => Number(line.slice(9, 12)));
    // A failure body opens with a boundary and MIME headers, so a short slice
    // shows the envelope and stops before the message saying why.
    const firstError = (text.match(/\{"error":[\s\S]{0,400}/) || [''])[0];
    return {
      ok: res.ok,
      status: res.status,
      done: codes.filter((c) => c >= 200 && c < 300).length,
      failed: codes.filter((c) => c < 200 || c >= 300).length,
      why: firstError || text.slice(0, 400),
    };
  };

  // Run `ops` in chunks of BATCH_SIZE, stopping the moment a chunk stops making
  // progress: one that wrote nothing will not do better on the next attempt,
  // and looping over it buries the reason.
  const spBatchAll = async (label, ops) => {
    let done = 0;
    for (let at = 0; at < ops.length; at += BATCH_SIZE) {
      const chunk = ops.slice(at, at + BATCH_SIZE);
      const r = await spBatch(chunk);
      if (!r.ok) {
        log('FAIL', `${label}: $batch HTTP ${r.status}. ${r.why}`);
        break;
      }
      if (r.failed) log('FAIL', `${label}: ${r.failed} operation(s) failed. ${r.why}`);
      if (r.done === 0) {
        log('FAIL', `${label}: a chunk of ${chunk.length} wrote nothing. ${r.why}`);
        break;
      }
      done += r.done;
      log('OK', `${label}: ${done} of ${ops.length}.`);
    }
    return done;
  };

  const parentCount = async () => {
    const r = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')?$select=ItemCount`);
    return readFailed(r) ? -1 : r.body.ItemCount;
  };

  // What did the batch ACTUALLY do? Read it out of the multipart response, one
  // status line per inner operation.
  //
  // NOT from SP.List.ItemCount, and this cost a run: ItemCount LAGS. Line ~420
  // below already says so, from three earlier runs that each read 0 on a list
  // holding a row. A first batch of 500 was created correctly, every inner
  // operation answered 201, and an ItemCount read immediately afterwards still
  // said 1. The loader called that "inner operations failed" and stopped.
  //
  // Where to resume from, and it must NOT be ItemCount. Reading the highest id
  // asks the ITEMS, which is what line ~420 below already concluded for the
  // same reason: ItemCount lags, and a resume cursor taken from it restarts the
  // load instead of continuing it. Harmless to the measurement (the target
  // only has to be large, and the child rows all point at item 1 whatever else
  // is in here), but a third pass would add another full target's worth.
  //
  // $orderby=Id desc is index-backed, so it is served past the threshold where
  // an unordered count would not be.
  const parentHighId = async () => {
    const r = await spGet(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/items?$select=Id&$top=1&$orderby=Id desc`);
    return (!readFailed(r) && r.body.value && r.body.value.length)
      ? r.body.value[0].Id : 0;
  };

  // Same reason on a measuring-only paste: ItemCount can under-report a list
  // that was grown moments ago, and this number is stamped into the LOOKID /
  // LOOCAM / LOOPRJ questions as the target size.
  let parentSize = Math.max(await parentCount(), await parentHighId());
  if (LOAD_PARENT) {
    const startedAt = await parentHighId();
    let made = 0;
    log('INFO',
        `Lookup target '${PARENT}': highest id ${startedAt} (ItemCount reads ` +
        `${parentSize}); growing to ${PARENT_TARGET}.`);
    const url = `${WEB}/_api/web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/items`;
    const wanted = [];
    for (let row = startedAt + 1; row <= PARENT_TARGET; row += 1) {
      wanted.push({
        method: 'POST',
        url,
        body: { Title: `Parent ${String(row).padStart(6, '0')}` },
      });
    }
    made = await spBatchAll(`lookup target '${PARENT}'`, wanted);
    // ItemCount is informational: it may still lag what was just written, which
    // is why `made` and not this decides whether the load succeeded. The
    // reported size takes the largest of the three, so the figure stamped into
    // the lookup questions never UNDER-states the target. Reading "into a
    // 1-item target" on a list holding thousands is how a measurement gets
    // filed against the wrong condition.
    const settled = await parentCount();
    parentSize = Math.max(settled, startedAt + made, await parentHighId());
    log(startedAt + made >= PARENT_TARGET ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `Lookup target '${PARENT}': created ${made} this run, ${startedAt + made} ` +
        `of ${PARENT_TARGET} by count of successful writes. ItemCount reads ` +
        `${settled}${settled < startedAt + made ? ' (lagging, not a failure)' : ''}.`);
  }

{% include "_threshold_rows.js.j2" %}

  // Which user id the Person population gets. OWNER_ID when pinned, otherwise
  // the account doing the pasting, safe here in a way it is NOT for the
  // measuring half, because this is CHOOSING the value to store rather than
  // guessing one to match. Whatever goes in, the discovery step later reads it
  // back off the rows.
  const seedUser = await spGet('web/currentuser?$select=Id');
  const ownerSeed = OWNER_ID
    || ((!readFailed(seedUser) && seedUser.body.Id) ? seedUser.body.Id : 0);

  // ---- Build the fixture, in phases, from here -------------------------
  // Creates only the rows the list does not have, so raising TEST_PHASE
  // continues an existing fixture rather than duplicating it.
  //
  // The resume point is the highest Title, not ItemCount, for the reason the
  // lookup target already needed: ItemCount lags a write by long enough to
  // read zero on a list that just took 500 rows, and a cursor from it restarts
  // the load. Titles are `Row NNNNNN` in load order and unique, so the largest
  // one is exactly how far the fixture got.
  if (TEST_PHASE > 0) {
    const target = CHECKPOINTS[Math.min(TEST_PHASE, CHECKPOINTS.length) - 1];
    const highest = await spGet(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/items?$select=Title&$top=1&$orderby=Id desc`);
    const lastTitle = (!readFailed(highest) && highest.body.value
                       && highest.body.value.length)
      ? highest.body.value[0].Title : '';
    const from = /^Row \d{6}$/.test(lastTitle || '')
      ? Number(lastTitle.slice(4)) + 1 : 1;
    if (from > target) {
      log('OK', `Phase ${TEST_PHASE}: list already holds ${from - 1} row(s), ` +
                `target ${target}. Nothing to create.`);
    } else if (!ownerSeed || !parentItemId) {
      log('FAIL',
          `Phase ${TEST_PHASE}: need a site user id and a parent item id before ` +
          'creating rows. The Person and Lookup populations would load blank ' +
          'and PERSID / LOOKID would be about an empty column.');
    } else {
      log('INFO', `Phase ${TEST_PHASE}: creating rows ${from}..${target} as ${ownerSeed}.`);
      const url = `${WEB}/_api/web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/items`;
      const ops = [];
      for (let row = from; row <= target; row += 1) {
        ops.push({ method: 'POST', url, body: thresholdRow(row, ownerSeed, parentItemId) });
      }
      const made = await spBatchAll(`phase ${TEST_PHASE}`, ops);
      log(made === ops.length ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
          `Phase ${TEST_PHASE}: created ${made} of ${ops.length} row(s). ` +
          'RUNCNT below checks the list against a declared checkpoint; nothing ' +
          'is measured unless it lands exactly.');
    }
  }

  // ---- Views ----------------------------------------------------------
  // CAML, because a view filter is not an OData filter. These exist so the
  // operator can OPEN them and report what a human sees; NULCAM below measures
  // the same CAML programmatically. Three sources for one question, because
  // they are three code paths.
  //
  // Paged: true. The RowLimit element's documentation is explicit that an
  // unspecified Paged makes the limit ABSOLUTE with no "show more" link, which
  // is not the shape the template library ships. ViewFields is NOT passed in
  // the create body: the harness sends odata=nometadata, where the verbose
  // {results: []} collection shape is a type mismatch, and this repo's
  // deployer adds fields one at a time afterwards for the same reason.
  const VIEW_FIELDS = ['Title', 'Bucket', 'Shadow', 'ClosedAt'];
  const VIEWS = [
    ['Cmp indexed',
     `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Bucket'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq></Where>`],
    ['Null indexed',
     "<Where><IsNull><FieldRef Name='ClosedAt'/></IsNull></Where>"],
    ['Cmp unindexed',
     `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Shadow'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq></Where>`],
    ['Sort bait',
     "<OrderBy><FieldRef Name='SortBait'/></OrderBy>"],
  ];
  const viewsPath = `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/views`;
  for (const [title, query] of VIEWS) {
    if ((await spGet(`${viewsPath}/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')?$select=Id`)).ok) continue;
    let digest = await getDigest();
    const made = await spPost(viewsPath, {
      Title: title, ViewQuery: query, RowLimit: 30, Paged: true, PersonalView: false,
    }, digest);
    if (!made.ok) {
      log('FAIL', `view '${title}' FAILED HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
      continue;
    }
    for (const field of VIEW_FIELDS) {
      digest = await getDigest();
      await spPost(
        `${viewsPath}/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')/viewfields/addviewfield('${field}')`,
        {}, digest);
    }
    log('OK', `view '${title}' created with ${VIEW_FIELDS.length} field(s)`);
  }

  // ---- Handshake ------------------------------------------------------
  const me = await spGet('web/currentuser?$select=Id,LoginName');
  const whoami = readFailed(me)
    ? '(currentuser unreadable)'
    : `${me.body.Id} / ${me.body.LoginName}`;
  log('INFO', '=============== FEED THESE TO THE GENERATOR ===============');
  log('INFO', `  .venv/Scripts/python.exe test/manual/make_threshold_rows.py \\`);
  log('INFO', `      --owner-id ${readFailed(me) ? 'UNREADABLE' : me.body.Id} --parent-id ${parentItemId}`);
  log('INFO', `  (--parent-id ${parentItemId} is the measured first item of '${PARENT}')`);
  log('INFO', `  Then set OWNER_ID in this probe to the same value.`);
  log('INFO', `  ListItemEntityTypeFullName: ${main.ListItemEntityTypeFullName}`);
  log('INFO', '  Some batch-create flows need that as the __metadata type.');
  log('INFO', '===========================================================');

  // ---- Snapshot -------------------------------------------------------
  const live = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')?$select=ItemCount`);
  const count = readFailed(live) ? -1 : live.body.ItemCount;
  const stamp = `${count} item(s)${RUN_LABEL ? `, run '${RUN_LABEL}'` : ''}`;
  log('INFO', `=== SNAPSHOT at ${stamp}, as ${whoami} ===`);
  if (!RUN_LABEL) {
    log('INFO', 'RUN_LABEL is unset. Step 6 of the run plan needs it: the');
    log('INFO', 'before/after-sort pair is taken at the SAME row count, and');
    log('INFO', 'nothing else distinguishes the two transcripts.');
  }

  // SharePoint's batch API is explicitly non-transactional ("if any of the
  // child operations fails, the others still complete and aren't rolled back"),
  // so a half-loaded batch is SILENT, and every observation below would be
  // filed under a row count the list never actually reached.
  //
  // 0 gets its own outcome: the provisioning run legitimately has an empty
  // list, and crying "a batch load may have partly failed" at every operator's
  // first paste is how a real warning stops being read.
  const onCheckpoint = CHECKPOINTS.includes(count);
  record(
    'RUNCNT', 'Live ItemCount matches a declared checkpoint',
    count < 0 ? 'NOT ESTABLISHED'
      : count === 0 ? 'NOT LOADED YET'
      : onCheckpoint ? 'ON CHECKPOINT' : 'OFF CHECKPOINT',
    `ItemCount=${count}; checkpoints are ${CHECKPOINTS.join(', ')}` + (
      (onCheckpoint || count <= 0) ? '' :
      '. A batch load may have partly failed, or a file was loaded twice. ' +
      'EVERY ROW BELOW IS SUSPECT. Reconcile the list first.'
    ),
  );

  // ---- Are the controls actually controls? ----------------------------
  // The whole experiment is an indexed-versus-unindexed comparison, so a MERGE
  // that returned 200 without taking effect would mislabel every row in the
  // table. Read the flags back rather than trusting the status code. The
  // deployer's own index step is documented as "verified by readback" for
  // exactly this reason.
  // ---- Restore the negative control before reading anything -----------
  // Run of 2026-08-17: Shadow came back Indexed=true, AutoIndexed=true, so
  // SharePoint had indexed it between runs and the negative control had
  // expired. Without the control, "the indexed one was served" says nothing:
  // the unindexed twin is what shows the throttle is enforced at this size.
  //
  // Attempted before IDXSET reads the flags, and REPORTED rather than
  // assumed. If SharePoint re-indexes it during the run, or refuses the
  // clear, the table below is void and IDXSET is what says so.
  const clearControl = async () => {
    const before = await readField('Shadow');
    const wasIndexed = !readFailed(before) && before.body.Indexed === true;
    if (!wasIndexed) {
      record('IDXCLR', 'Can the negative control be un-indexed again?',
             'NOT NEEDED',
             `[${stamp}] Shadow is already unindexed, so the negative control `
             + 'stands and nothing was written.');
      return;
    }
    if (!ALLOW_WRITES) {
      record('IDXCLR', 'Can the negative control be un-indexed again?',
             'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] Shadow is indexed and ALLOW_WRITES is false, so the `
             + 'control cannot be restored. Every indexed-versus-unindexed row '
             + 'is void until it is.');
      return;
    }
    const cleared = await setIndexed('Shadow', false);
    const after = await readField('Shadow');
    const nowIndexed = readFailed(after) || after.body.Indexed !== false;
    record('IDXCLR', 'Can the negative control be un-indexed again?',
           nowIndexed ? 'DID NOT STICK' : 'CLEARED',
           `[${stamp}] Shadow read Indexed=true; MERGE Indexed:false -> HTTP `
           + `${cleared.status}; readback Indexed=`
           + `${readFailed(after) ? 'unreadable' : after.body.Indexed}. `
           + (nowIndexed
             ? 'So the negative control cannot be restored on this list and '
               + 'every indexed-versus-unindexed row here is void. Whether '
               + 'SharePoint re-applies it immediately or the write was '
               + 'refused, the comparison is gone either way.'
             : 'The control is restored for this run. It was SharePoint that '
               + 'set the flag, not this probe, so expect it back: read '
               + 'FLAGS at the end of the run before trusting any pair.'));
  };
  await clearControl();

  const flags = [];
  const wrong = [];
  for (const [name, shouldBeIndexed] of schemas) {
    // null means this column's index state is an OBSERVATION, not a control.
    // Only the columns a filter actually reads can void the table.
    const asserted = shouldBeIndexed !== null;
    const field = await readField(name);
    if (readFailed(field) || typeof field.body.Indexed !== 'boolean') {
      flags.push(`${name}=unreadable`);
      if (asserted) wrong.push(`${name} (could not read)`);
      continue;
    }
    const auto = typeof field.body.AutoIndexed === 'boolean'
      ? field.body.AutoIndexed : '(not exposed)';
    flags.push(`${name}: Indexed=${field.body.Indexed}, AutoIndexed=${auto}`
               + (asserted ? '' : ' (observed, not asserted)'));
    if (asserted && field.body.Indexed !== shouldBeIndexed) {
      wrong.push(`${name} (wanted ${shouldBeIndexed}, is ${field.body.Indexed})`);
    }
  }
  record(
    'IDXSET', 'The intended indexes are set and the controls are not',
    wrong.length ? 'MISLABELLED, TABLE VOID' : 'CONFIRMED',
    wrong.length
      ? `[${stamp}] ${wrong.join('; ')}. Every indexed/unindexed row below is ` +
        'about a column whose index state is not what it claims.'
      : `[${stamp}] Bucket, ClosedAt, Owner, Parent indexed; Shadow not. ` +
        'SortBait is excluded on purpose: it is the auto-index bait, so it is ' +
        'EXPECTED to turn over, and no filter here reads it.',
  );
  record(
    'FLAGS', 'Indexed/AutoIndexed for all six columns',
    flags.length ? 'OBSERVED' : 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
    `[${stamp}] ${flags.join(' | ')}. AutoIndexed means nothing on its own: ` +
    'only a CHANGE across the before/after-sort pair establishes what sets it. ' +
    'Shadow is here because if it silently gains an index the negative control ' +
    'has expired and any CMPUNI result below is uninterpretable.',
  );

  // ---- Filters --------------------------------------------------------
  const items = `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/items`;

  // Classify from the error body, so a row is self-describing in a single
  // transcript. A malformed $filter and a threshold refusal both come back as
  // a 4xx/5xx that isRefusal() reads as REFUSED, and conclusions get drawn
  // from the LAST transcript, where a filter that was broken at every
  // checkpoint would read as a threshold refusal.
  const classify = (r) => {
    const body = r.body ? JSON.stringify(r.body) : '';
    // TRANSIENT FIRST, before the body is inspected. A 429 or 408 whose body
    // happens to mention the threshold would otherwise be recorded as a
    // threshold REFUSAL, turning a throttle, which is about the moment, into
    // an answered verdict about the platform. That is a wrong finding, not a
    // missing one, and it would be indistinguishable from a real refusal in
    // the transcript.
    if (r.status === 429 || r.status === 408) return 'NOT ESTABLISHED (throttled)';
    if (/exceeds the list view threshold|SPQueryThrottledException/i.test(body)) {
      return 'REFUSED (threshold)';
    }
    if (isRefusal(r.status)) return 'REFUSED (request rejected; check the body)';
    return 'NOT ESTABLISHED';
  };

  // expectedRows null means "this filter is not selectivity-matched"; the row
  // then reports what it matched without claiming comparability.
  const judge = (r, expectedRows) => {
    if (!r.ok) return classify(r);
    const matched = (r.body && r.body.value) ? r.body.value.length : -1;
    if (expectedRows === null) return `SERVED (${matched} row(s))`;
    // A filter that matches NOTHING returns 200 and would otherwise read as a
    // clean SERVED, which is how an empty column, or a Flow that dropped a
    // field, becomes "an index on this type does avert the threshold".
    //
    // SHORT gets its own outcome, separate from a plain mismatch. HTTP 200
    // carrying FEWER rows than the data holds is the documented failure this
    // whole check exists to warn about: past the threshold, a query no index
    // can serve falls back to returning up to the newest 1,250 items (or none)
    // rather than reporting an error. Filing that as "NOT ESTABLISHED" reads
    // as a question nobody asked, when it may be the answer.
    //
    // It is not self-certifying: a short count is ALSO what a fixture that
    // never held the rows looks like. SEEDCK separates the two by reading the
    // data back through a filter already known to be served.
    if (matched < expectedRows) {
      return `SHORT (${matched} of ${expectedRows}, HTTP 200), truncation or `
             + 'fixture fault; SEEDCK separates them';
    }
    if (matched !== expectedRows) {
      return `NOT ESTABLISHED (matched ${matched}, expected ${expectedRows})`;
    }
    return `SERVED (${matched} row(s), as expected)`;
  };

  const ask = async (id, question, filter, expectedRows) => {
    const r = await spGet(
      `${items}?$select=Id&$top=${PAGE}&$filter=${encodeURIComponent(filter)}`);
    const body = r.body ? JSON.stringify(r.body).slice(0, 300) : '(no JSON body)';
    // A failure at the FIRST checkpoint means the filter is wrong, not that
    // SharePoint refused it. Bounded to checkpoint 1 only: the spec chose
    // 4,900 precisely because the effective threshold is documented as
    // variable, so annotating a refusal there as "broken" would libel a real
    // finding.
    const suspect = count > 0 && count <= CHECKPOINTS[0] && !r.ok;
    record(
      id, question, judge(r, expectedRows),
      `[${stamp}] $filter=${filter}, HTTP ${r.status}: ${body}` + (
        suspect
          ? '  <-- FAILED AT THE FIRST CHECKPOINT, so this is a broken filter, not evidence.'
          : ''
      ),
    );
  };

  // CAML, via the same method the modern UI uses to render a view. This is the
  // path analysis/checks/_views.py actually asks about.
  // viewFields decides whether the query pays for a JOIN, and for a lookup
  // column that is the whole question. Filtering on a lookup reads the stored
  // id, which sits on the row; PROJECTING one joins to the target list across
  // the result set. Microsoft's rule about indexed lookup columns is about the
  // second, so a probe that only ever filters cannot speak to it, and a CAML
  // <View> with no <ViewFields> is a shape no real view has.
  //
  // Left empty for every question that is not about a lookup, so those stay
  // exactly the query they were when they were answered.
  const askCaml = async (id, question, where, expectedRows, viewFields = []) => {
    const digest = await getDigest();
    const fields = viewFields.length
      ? `<ViewFields>${viewFields.map((f) => `<FieldRef Name='${f}'/>`).join('')}</ViewFields>`
      : '';
    const viewXml =
      `<View><Query>${where}</Query>${fields}<RowLimit>${PAGE}</RowLimit></View>`;
    const r = await spPost(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/RenderListDataAsStream`,
      { parameters: { ViewXml: viewXml } }, digest);
    const rows = (r.ok && r.body && Array.isArray(r.body.Row)) ? r.body.Row.length : -1;
    let outcome;
    if (!r.ok) {
      outcome = classify(r);
    } else if (rows < 0) {
      outcome = 'NOT ESTABLISHED (no Row array in the response)';
    } else if (expectedRows !== null && rows < expectedRows) {
      // See judge() for why SHORT is not the same as NOT ESTABLISHED.
      outcome = `SHORT (${rows} of ${expectedRows}, HTTP 200), truncation or `
                + 'fixture fault; SEEDCK separates them';
    } else if (expectedRows !== null && rows !== expectedRows) {
      outcome = `NOT ESTABLISHED (matched ${rows}, expected ${expectedRows})`;
    } else {
      outcome = `SERVED (${rows} row(s), as expected)`;
    }
    // Did the JOIN actually happen? Asking for a lookup column in <ViewFields>
    // is not proof that SharePoint returned it: RenderListDataAsStream sends a
    // standard set of fields whatever is requested, and the evidence is sliced
    // at 300 characters, which stops inside that preamble. So a projecting
    // query whose ViewFields were quietly ignored looks EXACTLY like one that
    // paid for the join and was served, and the second reading would retire a
    // Microsoft-documented rule on a query that never joined anything.
    //
    // Check the returned row for each requested field by key prefix, because a
    // lookup comes back decorated (`Parent`, `Parent.`, `ParentId`, and so on)
    // rather than under the bare name.
    // WHICH rows came back, not just how many. A short result's row count says
    // something was dropped; the span says WHERE the boundary fell, and that is
    // the difference between "SharePoint truncated" and a description of how.
    //
    // Titles are `Row NNNNNN` in load order, so first and last locate the
    // window. A result that stops near row 5,000 is a scan bounded at the
    // threshold returning what it found; one that stops elsewhere is not.
    let span = '';
    if (r.ok && r.body && Array.isArray(r.body.Row) && r.body.Row.length) {
      const titles = r.body.Row.map((row) => row.Title).filter(Boolean);
      if (titles.length) {
        span = ` SPAN: ${titles[0]} .. ${titles[titles.length - 1]}.`;
      }
    }
    let projected = '';
    if (viewFields.length) {
      const first = (r.ok && r.body && Array.isArray(r.body.Row) && r.body.Row[0])
        ? Object.keys(r.body.Row[0]) : [];
      projected = ' PROJECTED: ' + viewFields.map((f) => {
        const hit = first.filter((k) => k === f || k.startsWith(`${f}.`)
                                        || k === `${f}Id`);
        return `${f}=${hit.length ? hit.join('/') : 'ABSENT'}`;
      }).join(', ') + (
        first.length ? '' : ' (no row to inspect)'
      ) + '. An ABSENT lookup means ViewFields was ignored and NO join was paid '
        + 'for, so this row says nothing the non-projecting one did not.';
    }
    record(id, question, outcome,
           `[${stamp}] CAML ${where}, HTTP ${r.status}:${span}${projected} ` +
           `${(r.text || '').slice(0, 300)}`);
  };

  // ---- Can the lookup TARGET still be picked from? --------------------
  // A view reads a lookup into a large list fine. The NEW-ITEM FORM does not:
  // past 5,000 in the target, the picker refuses with "This is a lookup column
  // that displays data from another list that currently exceeds the List View
  // Threshold defined by the administrator (5000)." Nobody can set the column.
  //
  // The picker has to ENUMERATE the target, and that is what breaches, so
  // changing which field it displays cannot help by itself. What might is an
  // index on the displayed column, making the enumeration index-served. These
  // ask the query shapes a picker needs, against the grown target, and the
  // ShowField is indexed between the two halves so the pair differs in one
  // thing.
  const targetItems = `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/items`;
  const askTarget = async (id, question, query) => {
    const r = await spGet(`${targetItems}?${query}`);
    const rows = (r.ok && r.body && r.body.value) ? r.body.value.length : -1;
    record(id, question, r.ok ? `SERVED (${rows} row(s))` : classify(r),
           `[target holds ${parentSize}] ?${query}, HTTP ${r.status}: ` +
           `${r.body ? JSON.stringify(r.body).slice(0, 200) : '(no JSON body)'}`);
  };

  // Is the ShowField indexed? Read it rather than assume. The answer decides
  // how the three rows below read, exactly as IDXSET does for the main table.
  const showField = await spGet(
    `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/fields/getbyinternalnameortitle('Title')`);
  const showIndexed = !readFailed(showField) && showField.body.Indexed === true;
  record('TGTIDX', "The lookup target's ShowField index state",
         typeof (showField.body || {}).Indexed === 'boolean' ? 'OBSERVED' : 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
         `[target holds ${parentSize}] Title: Indexed=${showIndexed}. Set ` +
         'INDEX_SHOWFIELD and re-paste to take the other half of the pair; the ' +
         'three rows below are only comparable across two runs that differ here.');

  await askTarget('TGTENU', 'Enumerate the lookup target, unordered',
                  '$select=Id,Title&$top=100');
  await askTarget('TGTORD', 'Enumerate the lookup target ordered by ShowField',
                  '$select=Id,Title&$top=100&$orderby=Title asc');
  await askTarget('TGTPFX', 'Type-ahead against the lookup target',
                  `$select=Id,Title&$top=100&$filter=${encodeURIComponent("startswith(Title,'Parent 0001')")}`);

  // ---- Can a conditional ShowField make a large target pickable? -------
  // The idea under test: give the target a calculated column that returns the
  // Title for the rows that should be offered and "" for the rest, driven by an
  // INDEXED status column, and point the lookup's ShowField at it.
  //
  // The mechanism it needs is that "" rows cost nothing. They do not. The
  // threshold counts rows SCANNED, not rows displayed, so a calculated column
  // evaluating to "" on 5,500 of 6,500 still has 6,500 rows to read, and it
  // cannot carry an index to avoid that, because CALCIDX establishes that a
  // Calculated column is not indexable. Both halves are measured rather than
  // argued: whether the column can be a ShowField at all, and whether ordering
  // by it is served.
  //
  // TGTSFO is the variant that can work, and it needs no new column: narrow
  // with an INDEXED filter first, then order. If the index makes the ordered
  // enumeration servable, the workaround is a target that is FILTERABLE to
  // under the threshold by an indexed column, not one whose labels are blank.
  const targetFields = `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/fields`;
  const pickLabel = await spGet(
    `${targetFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('PickLabel')`);
  if (readFailed(pickLabel)) {
    const made = await spPost(`${targetFields}/createfieldasxml`, {
      parameters: {
        SchemaXml:
          '<Field Type="Calculated" DisplayName="PickLabel" Name="PickLabel" '
          + 'ResultType="Text"><Formula>=IF(Title="","",Title)</Formula>'
          + '<FieldRefs><FieldRef Name="Title"/></FieldRefs></Field>',
        Options: 8,
      },
    }, await getDigest());
    log(made.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `target PickLabel ${made.ok ? 'created' : `FAILED HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 200)}`}`);
  }

  // Provisioning is the whole question here: a refusal answers it outright.
  const byLabel = await spGet(
    `${fieldsPath}/getbyinternalnameortitle('ParentLabel')`);
  if (readFailed(byLabel)) {
    const made = await spPost(`${fieldsPath}/createfieldasxml`, {
      parameters: {
        SchemaXml:
          '<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="ParentLabel" Name="ParentLabel" '
          + `List="{${parent.Id}}" ShowField="PickLabel"/>`,
        Options: 8,
      },
    }, await getDigest());
    record('LKPCAL', 'Can a Lookup show a CALCULATED column from its target?',
           made.ok ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'REFUSED',
           `HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}. A refusal ends the ` +
           'conditional-label idea here; an acceptance leaves TGTCAL to say ' +
           'whether the picker could actually enumerate by it.');
  } else {
    record('LKPCAL', 'Can a Lookup show a CALCULATED column from its target?',
           'ACCEPTED', 'The field already exists from an earlier paste.');
  }

  await askTarget('TGTCAL', 'Order the lookup target by the CALCULATED label',
                  '$select=Id,PickLabel&$top=100&$orderby=PickLabel asc');

  // ---- The conditional label, driven by an INDEXED status column -------
  // PickLabel above is a plain passthrough and does NOT test this. The scheme
  // being asked about is: an INDEXED status column, a calculated column that
  // reads it and returns the Title or "", and the lookup pointed at that.
  //
  // Built as specified rather than shortcut, because the reason to expect a
  // particular answer is not a reason to measure something else. PickStatus is
  // a real indexed Choice, PickCond really reads it, and both are separate
  // names so the passthrough pair above stays intact for comparison.
  const targetStatus = await spGet(
    `${targetFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('PickStatus')`);
  if (readFailed(targetStatus)) {
    const made = await spPost(`${targetFields}/createfieldasxml`, {
      parameters: {
        SchemaXml:
          '<Field Type="Choice" DisplayName="PickStatus" Name="PickStatus">'
          + '<CHOICES><CHOICE>Active</CHOICE><CHOICE>Archived</CHOICE></CHOICES>'
          + '</Field>',
        Options: 8,
      },
    }, await getDigest());
    log(made.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `target PickStatus ${made.ok ? 'created' : `FAILED HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 200)}`}`);
    if (made.ok) {
      const flagged = await spPost(
        `${targetFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('PickStatus')`,
        { __metadata: { type: 'SP.Field' }, Indexed: true }, await getDigest(),
        {
          Accept: 'application/json;odata=verbose',
          'Content-Type': 'application/json;odata=verbose',
          'X-HTTP-Method': 'MERGE',
          'IF-MATCH': '*',
        });
      log(flagged.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL', `target PickStatus: Indexed=true requested`);
    }
  }
  const pickCond = await spGet(`${targetFields}/getbyinternalnameortitle('PickCond')`);
  if (readFailed(pickCond)) {
    const made = await spPost(`${targetFields}/createfieldasxml`, {
      parameters: {
        SchemaXml:
          '<Field Type="Calculated" DisplayName="PickCond" Name="PickCond" '
          + 'ResultType="Text"><Formula>=IF(PickStatus="Active",Title,"")</Formula>'
          + '<FieldRefs><FieldRef Name="PickStatus"/><FieldRef Name="Title"/>'
          + '</FieldRefs></Field>',
        Options: 8,
      },
    }, await getDigest());
    log(made.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `target PickCond ${made.ok ? 'created' : `FAILED HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 200)}`}`);
  }

  // Mark a bounded slice Active, so `PickStatus eq 'Active'` is an INDEXED
  // filter selecting well under the threshold and PickCond is non-empty on
  // exactly those rows. Batched MERGEs by id, which do not go through the
  // threshold. The status flags whether the readback below means anything.
  const statusActive = await spGet(
    `${targetItems}?$select=Id&$top=1&$filter=${encodeURIComponent("PickStatus eq 'Active'")}`);
  const alreadyActive = !readFailed(statusActive) && statusActive.body.value
                        && statusActive.body.value.length > 0;
  if (SEED_TARGET_STATUS && !alreadyActive) {
    const url = `${WEB}/_api/web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/items`;
    const stamps = [];
    for (let id = 1; id <= TARGET_ACTIVE; id += 1) {
      stamps.push({
        method: 'MERGE',
        url: `${url}(${id})`,
        verbose: true,
        body: {
          __metadata: { type: parent.ListItemEntityTypeFullName },
          PickStatus: 'Active',
        },
      });
    }
    await spBatchAll('PickStatus', stamps);
  }
  const activeNow = await spGet(
    `${targetItems}?$select=Id&$top=${PAGE}&$filter=${encodeURIComponent("PickStatus eq 'Active'")}`);
  const activeCount = (!readFailed(activeNow) && activeNow.body.value)
    ? activeNow.body.value.length : -1;
  record('TGTSTA', "The INDEXED status filter on the lookup target",
         activeCount > 0 ? `SERVED (${activeCount} row(s), capped at ${PAGE})`
           : classify(activeNow),
         `[target holds ${parentSize}] $filter=PickStatus eq 'Active', HTTP ` +
         `${activeNow.status}. ${activeCount > 0 ? '' : 'Set SEED_TARGET_STATUS ' +
         'and re-paste: with nothing Active, PickCond is "" on every row and ' +
         'TGTCND / TGTFLT are about an empty column.'}`);

  // ---- The endpoint the PICKER actually calls --------------------------
  // Everything else here approximates what the new-item form does. This is the
  // call itself, taken from the form's own console failure:
  //
  //   POST _api/web/GetListUsingPath(DecodedUrl=@v1)
  //        /GetLookupFieldChoices(targetFieldName='PickLabel',pagingInfo='')
  //   500  SPQueryThrottledException
  //
  // Asked once per ShowField so the three cases separate: an INDEXED text
  // column, a CALCULATED one, and the status-driven conditional. If the indexed
  // column is served where the others are refused, indexing the ShowField is
  // the whole fix for a large lookup target, which is what TGTORD and TGTPFX
  // already point at, on approximated queries rather than this one.
  //
  // pagingInfo is empty deliberately: that is what the form sends on first
  // open, which is the moment it fails.
  // The choices come back as a JSON STRING inside the `value` property, so the
  // body needs parsing twice. Reporting the count and the highest LookupId is
  // what makes a FILTERED picker distinguishable from an unfiltered one: the
  // target's Active rows are ids 1..TARGET_ACTIVE, so a maximum above that
  // means every row was offered whatever any view says.
  const pickerChoices = (text) => {
    try {
      const outer = JSON.parse(text);
      const inner = JSON.parse(outer.value);
      const list = (inner && inner.choices) || [];
      const ids = list.map((c) => c.LookupId).filter((n) => typeof n === 'number');
      return { n: list.length, max: ids.length ? Math.max(...ids) : -1 };
    } catch { return { n: -1, max: -1 }; }
  };

  const askPicker = async (id, question, field) => {
    const path = `/${WEB.split('/').slice(3).join('/')}/Lists/${PARENT}`;
    const r = await spPost(
      'web/GetListUsingPath(DecodedUrl=@v1)'
      + `/GetLookupFieldChoices(targetFieldName='${field}',pagingInfo='')`
      + `?@v1='${encodeURIComponent(path)}'`, {}, await getDigest());
    const threshold = /exceeds the list view threshold|SPQueryThrottledException/i
      .test(r.text || '');
    const got = r.ok ? pickerChoices(r.text) : { n: -1, max: -1 };
    record(id, question,
           r.ok ? `SERVED (${got.n} choice(s))` : threshold ? 'REFUSED (threshold)'
             : `REFUSED (HTTP ${r.status})`,
           `[target holds ${parentSize}] GetLookupFieldChoices ` +
           `targetFieldName='${field}', HTTP ${r.status}` +
           (r.ok ? `, highest LookupId ${got.max} (Active rows are 1..${TARGET_ACTIVE}` +
                   `, so anything above that is an UNFILTERED picker)` : '') +
           `: ${(r.text || '').slice(0, 220)}`);
  };
  await askPicker('PIKIDX', 'Picker choices on an INDEXED ShowField', 'Title');
  await askPicker('PIKCAL', 'Picker choices on a CALCULATED ShowField', 'PickLabel');
  await askPicker('PIKCND', 'Picker choices on the STATUS-DRIVEN label', 'PickCond');

  // ---- Can a FILTERED DEFAULT VIEW restrict what the picker offers? ----
  // The question behind this: a Lookup into a large operational list offers
  // every row, including the closed ones nobody should be picking. SharePoint
  // has no filtering attribute on a Lookup field (the Field element carries
  // List, ShowField, Mult and no way to restrict the choices), so the only
  // native lever left is whether the picker honours the TARGET's default view.
  //
  // WORTH KNOWING BEFORE READING ANY ADVICE ON THIS. The workaround the
  // community converged on is a calculated column in the SOURCE list that
  // returns a label only for the rows that should be offered and "" otherwise,
  // used as the lookup's ShowField, because a Lookup will not offer a blank
  // value. PIKCAL and PIKCND ARE that workaround, and past 5,000 rows in the
  // target both come back SPQueryThrottledException: a Calculated column cannot
  // be indexed (CALCIDX), so the picker's enumeration has to scan.
  //
  // So the standard answer works on a small list and fails on exactly the large
  // one that motivated asking. That ceiling is not in the advice, and this pair
  // is the measurement of it.
  //
  // If it does, a filtered default view on the target is a real, supported,
  // schema-expressible answer. If it does not, there is no native filtered
  // lookup at all and the remedy has to be a smaller target list.
  //
  // Decided on the highest LookupId returned, not on the count: the picker
  // pages, so a short list proves nothing. Active rows are ids 1..TARGET_ACTIVE.
  const activeView = 'Active only';
  const parentViews = `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/views`;
  if ((await spGet(`${parentViews}/getbytitle('${odata(activeView)}')?$select=Id`)).ok === false) {
    const made = await spPost(parentViews, {
      Title: activeView,
      ViewQuery: "<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='PickStatus'/>"
                 + "<Value Type='Choice'>Active</Value></Eq></Where>",
      RowLimit: 30, Paged: true, PersonalView: false,
    }, await getDigest());
    log(made.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `target view '${activeView}' ${made.ok ? 'created' : `FAILED HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 200)}`}`);
  }
  if (FILTER_TARGET_VIEW) {
    const promoted = await spPost(
      `${parentViews}/getbytitle('${odata(activeView)}')`,
      { __metadata: { type: 'SP.View' }, DefaultView: true }, await getDigest(),
      {
        Accept: 'application/json;odata=verbose',
        'Content-Type': 'application/json;odata=verbose',
        'X-HTTP-Method': 'MERGE',
        'IF-MATCH': '*',
      });
    log(promoted.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `target default view -> '${activeView}' ${promoted.ok
          ? 'requested. RE-PASTE to measure it'
          : `FAILED HTTP ${promoted.status} ${promoted.text.slice(0, 200)}`}`);
  }
  const defaulted = await spGet(
    `${parentViews}/getbytitle('${odata(activeView)}')?$select=DefaultView`);
  record('PIKVW', "Does the picker honour the target's DEFAULT VIEW filter?",
         readFailed(defaulted) ? 'NOT ESTABLISHED'
           : defaulted.body.DefaultView ? 'MEASURED (read PIKIDX above)'
           : 'NOT MEASURED (the filtered view is not default yet)',
         `[target holds ${parentSize}] '${activeView}' filters PickStatus='Active' ` +
         `and DefaultView reads ${readFailed(defaulted) ? '(unreadable)' : defaulted.body.DefaultView}. ` +
         'With it default, PIKIDX above decides it: a highest LookupId inside ' +
         `1..${TARGET_ACTIVE} means the picker honours the view and a native ` +
         'filtered lookup exists; anything higher means it does not, and the ' +
         'only native remedy is a smaller target list. Set FILTER_TARGET_VIEW, ' +
         'paste, then paste again to read PIKIDX under it.');

  await askTarget('TGTCND', 'Order the target by the STATUS-DRIVEN conditional label',
                  '$select=Id,PickCond&$top=100&$orderby=PickCond asc');
  await askTarget('TGTFLT', 'Type-ahead against the STATUS-DRIVEN conditional label',
                  '$select=Id,PickCond&$top=100'
                  + `&$filter=${encodeURIComponent("startswith(PickCond,'Parent')")}`);
  await askTarget('TGTSTO', 'INDEXED status filter first, THEN order by Title',
                  '$select=Id,Title,PickCond&$top=100'
                  + `&$filter=${encodeURIComponent("PickStatus eq 'Active'")}`
                  + '&$orderby=Title asc');
  await askTarget('TGTSFO', 'INDEXED filter first, THEN order by ShowField',
                  '$select=Id,Title&$top=100'
                  + `&$filter=${encodeURIComponent("startswith(Title,'Parent 0001')")}`
                  + '&$orderby=Title asc');

  if (INDEX_SHOWFIELD && !showIndexed) {
    const flagged = await spPost(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(PARENT)}')/fields/getbyinternalnameortitle('Title')`,
      { __metadata: { type: 'SP.Field' }, Indexed: true }, await getDigest(),
      {
        Accept: 'application/json;odata=verbose',
        'Content-Type': 'application/json;odata=verbose',
        'X-HTTP-Method': 'MERGE',
        'IF-MATCH': '*',
      });
    log(flagged.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `Target ShowField 'Title': Indexed=true ${flagged.ok
          ? 'requested. RE-PASTE to measure the indexed half'
          : `FAILED HTTP ${flagged.status} ${flagged.text.slice(0, 200)}`}`);
  }

  // ---- Which column types can carry an index at all? -------------------
  // A Calculated column is the obvious candidate for a cheap indexed ShowField,
  // and a Multi-line Text column is the one templates reach for by habit.
  // Whether either can be indexed is asserted in places and documented nowhere
  // first-party, so read the flag back rather than trust the MERGE's status.
  const canIndex = async (id, question, name, schemaXml) => {
    if (readFailed(await readField(name))) {
      const made = await addField(schemaXml);
      if (!made.ok) {
        record(id, question, 'REFUSED (cannot create the column)',
               `HTTP ${made.status}: ${made.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
        return;
      }
    }
    const flagged = await setIndexed(name);
    const after = await readField(name);
    const indexed = !readFailed(after) && after.body.Indexed === true;
    record(id, question,
           indexed ? 'INDEXABLE' : 'NOT INDEXABLE',
           `MERGE HTTP ${flagged.status}; Indexed reads ${indexed} on readback. ` +
           (flagged.ok && !indexed
             ? 'The MERGE was ACCEPTED and the flag did not take. A status code '
               + 'alone would have reported this column as indexed.'
             : '') + ` ${(flagged.text || '').slice(0, 200)}`);
  };
  await canIndex('CALCIDX', 'Can a Calculated column be indexed?', 'CalcBait',
                 '<Field Type="Calculated" DisplayName="CalcBait" Name="CalcBait" '
                 + 'ResultType="Text"><Formula>=CONCATENATE("c-",Title)</Formula>'
                 + '<FieldRefs><FieldRef Name="Title"/></FieldRefs></Field>');
  await canIndex('NOTEIDX', 'Can a Multi-line Text column be indexed?', 'NoteBait',
                 '<Field Type="Note" DisplayName="NoteBait" Name="NoteBait" '
                 + 'NumLines="3" RichText="FALSE"/>');

  // ---- Is anything below worth measuring at all? ----------------------
  // The first live run recorded thirteen answers against an EMPTY list: every
  // filter returned HTTP 200 with zero rows, judge() saw r.ok, and the summary
  // read "13 answered, 1 NOT established" one screen below IDXSET reporting
  // TABLE VOID. A filter over a list that is empty, off-checkpoint, or whose
  // index flags are not what they claim is not evidence about anything, so
  // the queries are not sent and every row says why.
  const blocked =
    count <= 0 ? 'the list is empty; load a CSV first'
    : !onCheckpoint ? `ItemCount ${count} is not a declared checkpoint`
    : wrong.length ? 'the index flags are not what they claim (see IDXSET)'
    : '';
  if (blocked) {
    for (const [id, question] of [
      ['CMPIDX', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Text (positive control)'],
      ['CMPUNI', 'OData comparison, UNINDEXED twin of Bucket (negative control)'],
      ['NULIDX', 'OData null test, INDEXED DateTime'],
      ['CMPCAM', 'CAML comparison, INDEXED Text'],
      ['NULCAM', 'CAML IsNull, INDEXED DateTime (the question as _views.py asks it)'],
      ['PERSID', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Person'],
      ['LOOKID', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Lookup'],
      ['SYSCRE', 'OData comparison on Created'],
      ['SYSMOD', 'OData comparison on Modified'],
      ['SYSAUT', 'OData comparison on Author'],
      ['SYSEDI', 'OData comparison on Editor'],
      ['PERCAM', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, LookupId form'],
      ['PERCAV', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, projected-value form'],
      ['LOOCAM', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Lookup, LookupId form'],
      ['NNIDX', 'CAML IsNotNull, INDEXED DateTime'],
      ['NNUNI', 'CAML IsNotNull, UNINDEXED DateTime (is an index NECESSARY?)'],
      ['SEEDCK', 'The seeded rows really do hold both values'],
      ['JOINMAX', 'How many Lookup columns can one view project?'],
      ['JOINSYS', 'Does Created By count as a join?'],
      ['JOINEDT', 'Does Modified By count as a join?'],
      ['JOINPER', 'Does a Person column count as a join?'],
      ['JOINPRJ', "Does a lookup's additional-field projection cost its own join?"],
      ['ANDIDX', 'CAML And, INDEXED condition FIRST'],
      ['ANDUNI', 'CAML And, UNINDEXED condition FIRST (same rows, reversed)'],
      ['CALCAM', 'CAML Eq on a CALCULATED column (OData refuses this by type)'],
      ['LOOPRJ', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Lookup, PROJECTING the lookup (the real view shape)'],
      ['PERPRJ', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, PROJECTING the person (the real view shape)'],
    ]) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] not measured: ${blocked}.`);
    }
    log('INFO', `No filter was measured: ${blocked}.`);
  }

  // Not an early return: CLEANUP_AT_END lives below, and an operator tearing
  // down after a failed load needs to reach it.
  // ---- Which user id do the rows actually carry? ----------------------
  // OWNER_ID is an operator constant, and it is reset to 0 every time the
  // template is re-rendered. Four Person questions have now been lost twice
  // that way. The probe ran, everything else answered, and PERSID / PERCAM /
  // PERCAV / PERPRJ came back "no filter was sent".
  //
  // So DISCOVER it instead, from the fixture. Reading it off a row is not the
  // thing the constant exists to prevent: the hazard was reading it from
  // web/currentuser, where a paste from a different account silently retargets
  // the filter at an id no row carries. A value taken from the DATA is the
  // value the data carries, by definition. It cannot be the wrong one.
  //
  // Owner is indexed and an indexed presence test is served past the threshold
  // (NULCAM / NNIDX), so this query works at 6,000. OWNER_ID still wins when
  // set, so an operator can pin it deliberately.
  let ownerId = OWNER_ID;
  if (!ownerId) {
    const found = await spGet(
      `${items}?$select=OwnerId&$top=1&$filter=${encodeURIComponent('OwnerId ne null')}`);
    const first = (!readFailed(found) && found.body.value && found.body.value[0]) || null;
    if (first && first.OwnerId) {
      ownerId = first.OwnerId;
      log('OK', `OWNER_ID was 0; discovered ${ownerId} from the fixture's own rows.`);
    } else {
      log('FAIL',
          `OWNER_ID is 0 and could not be discovered (HTTP ${found.status}). The ` +
          'four Person questions will not be sent. Set OWNER_ID by hand.');
    }
  }

  const measureFilters = async () => {
  // One row in a hundred, so this is the expected match count at any
  // checkpoint. Every offset is below 100 and every checkpoint is a multiple
  // of 100, so it is exact at all five, not only at 6,000.
  const matched = Math.floor(count / PER_HUNDRED);

  // ---- Seed the is-not-null pair --------------------------------------
  // Writes SEED_STAMP into NotNullIdx and NotNullUni on the SAME rows, so the
  // two columns hold identical data and differ only in the index.
  //
  // WHICH rows: the ones `Bucket eq 'Z'` returns. That filter is indexed and is
  // served past the threshold (established), so the set is both reachable at
  // 6,000 and exactly `matched` in size. Picking them any other way needs a
  // query that may itself be refused, and a seed set of unknown size makes
  // every count below unreadable. The correlation with Bucket is harmless: no
  // filter here combines the two columns.
  //
  // Both columns in ONE MERGE per row, so a partial failure cannot leave the
  // pair unequal, which would be the one outcome that silently invalidates the
  // comparison rather than failing it.
  //
  // Updating BY ID does not go through the list view threshold, so this works
  // at 6,000 where a filtered bulk update would not.
  const seedNotNull = async () => {
    const already = await spGet(
      `${items}?$select=Id&$top=${PAGE}&$filter=NotNullIdx ne null`);
    const seeded = (already.ok && already.body && already.body.value)
      ? already.body.value.length : -1;
    if (seeded === matched) {
      log('OK', `is-not-null pair already seeded on ${seeded} row(s).`);
      return true;
    }
    const targets = await spGet(
      `${items}?$select=Id&$top=${PAGE}&$filter=${encodeURIComponent(`Bucket eq '${RARE_BUCKET}'`)}`);
    const rows = (targets.ok && targets.body && targets.body.value) || [];
    if (rows.length !== matched) {
      log('FAIL',
          `Cannot seed the is-not-null pair: the Bucket='${RARE_BUCKET}' set ` +
          `returned ${rows.length} row(s), expected ${matched}. NNIDX and NNUNI ` +
          'are left unmeasured rather than measured against an unknown size.');
      return false;
    }
    // One digest per pass. A form digest lives about thirty minutes and 60
    // rows take seconds, so refreshing per row would double the request count
    // for nothing, and request count is what throttling counts.
    const digest = await getDigest();
    let written = 0;
    for (const row of rows) {
      const put = await spPost(`${items}(${row.Id})`, {
        __metadata: { type: main.ListItemEntityTypeFullName },
        NotNullIdx: SEED_STAMP,
        NotNullUni: SEED_STAMP,
      }, digest, {
        Accept: 'application/json;odata=verbose',
        'Content-Type': 'application/json;odata=verbose',
        'X-HTTP-Method': 'MERGE',
        'IF-MATCH': '*',
      });
      if (put.ok) written += 1;
      else if (written === 0) {
        // Report the FIRST failure's body. Sixty identical failures print sixty
        // times and bury the one line that says why.
        log('FAIL', `seed row ${row.Id}: HTTP ${put.status} ${put.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
      }
    }
    log(written === matched ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `is-not-null pair: seeded ${written} of ${matched} row(s).`);
    return written === matched;
  };
  const seedOk = await seedNotNull();

  // ---- Does the data actually say what NNUNI's count implies? ---------
  // NNUNI asks an UNINDEXED column for its 60 seeded rows. If it comes back
  // HTTP 200 holding fewer, there are exactly two explanations and they lead
  // opposite ways:
  //
  //   the rows ARE there  -> SharePoint silently returned a partial answer.
  //                          That is the documented threshold fallback and the
  //                          precise failure _views.py exists to warn about.
  //   the rows are NOT    -> the seed did not take on every row, and NNUNI is
  //                          a fixture fault reporting nothing about indexing.
  //
  // Counting through the UNINDEXED column cannot tell them apart. That is the
  // column under suspicion. So read the same rows through `Bucket eq 'Z'`,
  // which is indexed and established as served at this size, and select both
  // seeded columns. That count is about the DATA, not about any index.
  const verifySeed = async () => {
    const r = await spGet(
      `${items}?$select=Id,NotNullIdx,NotNullUni&$top=${PAGE}` +
      `&$filter=${encodeURIComponent(`Bucket eq '${RARE_BUCKET}'`)}`);
    if (readFailed(r) || !r.body.value) {
      record('SEEDCK', 'The seeded rows really do hold both values',
             'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] could not read the seed set back: HTTP ${r.status}.`);
      return;
    }
    const rows = r.body.value;
    const withIdx = rows.filter((row) => row.NotNullIdx).length;
    const withUni = rows.filter((row) => row.NotNullUni).length;
    const intact = rows.length === matched
                   && withIdx === matched && withUni === matched;
    record(
      'SEEDCK', 'The seeded rows really do hold both values',
      intact ? 'DATA INTACT' : 'DATA INCOMPLETE',
      `[${stamp}] read through the INDEXED Bucket filter: ${rows.length} row(s), ` +
      `NotNullIdx set on ${withIdx}, NotNullUni set on ${withUni}, expected ` +
      `${matched} of each. ` + (
        intact
          ? 'So any SHORT count from NNUNI is SharePoint returning a partial ' +
            'answer over an unindexed column, not missing data.'
          : 'So a SHORT count from NNUNI is a seeding failure and says nothing ' +
            'about indexing. Re-seed before reading NNIDX or NNUNI.'
      ),
    );
  };
  if (seedOk) await verifySeed();

  await ask('CMPIDX', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Text (positive control)',
            `Bucket eq '${RARE_BUCKET}'`, matched);
  await ask('CMPUNI', 'OData comparison, UNINDEXED twin of Bucket (negative control)',
            `Shadow eq '${RARE_BUCKET}'`, matched);
  // `eq null` is NOT in Microsoft's documented operator list for the SharePoint
  // REST service, and the only Microsoft-hosted statement found says OData
  // there does not support filtering on null, with CAML as the workaround. So
  // this row may well be a 400 at every checkpoint, which is why NULCAM
  // exists, and why the first checkpoint annotates a broken filter as broken.
  await ask('NULIDX', 'OData null test, INDEXED DateTime',
            'ClosedAt eq null', matched);
  // Not asked at all when OWNER_ID is unset. `OwnerId eq 0` matches nothing,
  // returns 200, and would be a SERVED, the exact false positive the constant
  // exists to prevent, so the query does not get to run.
  if (ownerId) {
    await ask('PERSID', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Person',
              `OwnerId eq ${ownerId}`, matched);
  } else {
    record('PERSID', 'OData comparison, INDEXED Person', 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           'No owner id: OWNER_ID is 0 and none could be discovered from the ' +
           'rows. Asking for `OwnerId eq 0` would match nothing, return HTTP ' +
           '200 and read as SERVED, so nothing was sent.');
  }
  await ask('LOOKID', `OData comparison, INDEXED Lookup (target holds ${parentSize})`,
            `ParentId eq ${parentItemId}`, matched);

  await askCaml(
    'CMPCAM', 'CAML comparison, INDEXED Text',
    `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Bucket'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq></Where>`,
    matched);
  await askCaml(
    'NULCAM', 'CAML IsNull, INDEXED DateTime (the question as _views.py asks it)',
    "<Where><IsNull><FieldRef Name='ClosedAt'/></IsNull></Where>", matched);


  // ---- How many JOINS may one view project? (issue #44) ----------------
  // A different ceiling from everything else here, and a nastier one: the list
  // view LOOKUP threshold is a property of the view's SHAPE, so it bites on a
  // list holding ten items and nothing a small deployment does avoids it.
  //
  // THE NUMBER FOR SHAREPOINT ONLINE IS 12. Not 8: that is the on-premises
  // pre-2013-CU default, and it comes from MaxQueryLookupFields, a farm
  // property that does not exist in SharePoint Online. This project profiles
  // SPO only, so a reader who "corrects" 12 back to 8 from a boundaries table
  // would be importing a server-only figure.
  //
  // And the SPO citation is unusually thin, which is the reason to measure it
  // rather than take it: no first-party SharePoint Online limits page
  // enumerates this threshold. The strongest Microsoft statement of it is in
  // the POWER QUERY connector documentation (the SharePoint Online list v2.0
  // connector "is subject to a maximum of 12 join operations per query"),
  // which then cross-references the on-premises boundaries table for the
  // definition. A number reached only by cross-reference is exactly the kind
  // this repository probes.
  //
  // Which column types count is thinner still: lookup, person/group and
  // managed metadata are named, and Created By / Modified By are widely
  // reported but nowhere documented. JOINSYS and JOINPER settle those.
  //
  // The filter stays at Bucket='Z' throughout (indexed, 60 rows, established
  // as served), so the ONLY variable across these rows is how many joins the
  // view projects. A failure is then attributable to joins and nothing else.
  const joinWhere =
    `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Bucket'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq></Where>`;
  const tryProject = async (fields) => {
    const viewXml =
      `<View><Query>${joinWhere}</Query>`
      + `<ViewFields>${fields.map((f) => `<FieldRef Name='${f}'/>`).join('')}</ViewFields>`
      + `<RowLimit>${PAGE}</RowLimit></View>`;
    const r = await spPost(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/RenderListDataAsStream`,
      { parameters: { ViewXml: viewXml } }, await getDigest());
    // Which of the requested fields actually came back. A view that renders is
    // only evidence about join cost if the column under test was PROJECTED.
    // ViewFields being silently dropped looks exactly like a join that was
    // free, and this fixture has already been caught by that once.
    const first = (r.ok && r.body && Array.isArray(r.body.Row) && r.body.Row[0])
      ? Object.keys(r.body.Row[0]) : [];
    const present = (name) => first.some(
      (k) => k === name || k.startsWith(`${name}.`) || k === `${name}Id`);
    return {
      ok: r.ok, status: r.status, text: (r.text || '').slice(0, 200), present,
      keys: first.length,
    };
  };

  if (PROBE_JOINS) {
    // Lookup columns with no data. Whether an EMPTY lookup still costs a join
    // is itself part of the question. The threshold is computed from the
    // view's shape, so it should, and if it does not that is the finding.
    for (let n = 1; n <= JOIN_COLUMNS; n += 1) {
      const name = `Join${n}`;
      if (readFailed(await readField(name))) {
        const made = await addField(
          `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="${name}" Name="${name}" `
          + `List="{${parent.Id}}" ShowField="Title"/>`);
        if (!made.ok) {
          log('FAIL', `join column ${name}: HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 200)}`);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    log('OK', `join probe: ${JOIN_COLUMNS} lookup column(s) available.`);
  }

  // Walk up one lookup at a time and record where rendering stops. Reported as
  // the last COUNT that worked, so the number is directly comparable with
  // Microsoft's "8" and "12" rather than being an off-by-one from a failure.
  const available = [];
  for (let n = 1; n <= JOIN_COLUMNS; n += 1) {
    if (!readFailed(await readField(`Join${n}`))) available.push(`Join${n}`);
  }
  if (!available.length) {
    record('JOINMAX', 'How many Lookup columns can one view project?',
           'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           `[${stamp}] no Join* columns exist. Set PROBE_JOINS and re-paste; ` +
           'this creates up to ' + JOIN_COLUMNS + ' empty lookups on the fixture.');
    for (const [id, question] of [
      ['JOINSYS', 'Does Created By count as a join?'],
      ['JOINEDT', 'Does Modified By count as a join?'],
      ['JOINPER', 'Does a Person column count as a join?'],
    ]) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] not measured: JOINMAX has no ceiling to work from.`);
    }
  } else {
    let worked = 0;
    let failure = '';
    for (let n = 1; n <= available.length; n += 1) {
      const r = await tryProject(['Title', ...available.slice(0, n)]);
      if (r.ok) { worked = n; continue; }
      failure = `HTTP ${r.status}: ${r.text}`;
      break;
    }
    record('JOINMAX', 'How many Lookup columns can one view project?',
           worked === available.length
             ? `NO CEILING FOUND (${worked} projected, all that exist)`
             : `CEILING ${worked}`,
           `[${stamp}] ${worked} lookup column(s) render; ${worked + 1} ` +
           `${failure ? `does not, ${failure}` : 'was not reachable'}. Microsoft ` +
           'documents 12 for SharePoint Online; 8 is the on-premises ' +
           'pre-CU default and does not apply here. The filter is the same ' +
           '60-row indexed Bucket query throughout, so joins are the only ' +
           'variable.');

    // AT the ceiling, plus exactly ONE suspect column. Below the ceiling the
    // test cannot discriminate: with a base of 11 against a ceiling of 12,
    // adding one column lands on 12 and renders whether or not that column is
    // a join, so a pass means nothing. Sitting at the ceiling makes the answer
    // binary: the extra column either pushes it to 13 and fails, or does not
    // count and it still renders.
    //
    // One suspect per row, not two. A base of 11 with Author AND Editor fails
    // if the pair contributes two, and says nothing about which one did it.
    const atCeiling = available.slice(0, worked);
    const suspects = [
      ['JOINSYS', 'Does Created By count as a join?', 'Author'],
      ['JOINEDT', 'Does Modified By count as a join?', 'Editor'],
      ['JOINPER', 'Does a Person column count as a join?', 'Owner'],
    ];
    for (const [id, question, column] of suspects) {
      if (!worked) {
        record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
               `[${stamp}] no ceiling was found, so there is nothing to sit at.`);
        continue;
      }
      const r = await tryProject(['Title', ...atCeiling, column]);
      record(id, question, r.ok ? 'NOT COUNTED' : 'COUNTED',
             `[${stamp}] ${worked} lookup(s) at the ceiling + ${column}, HTTP ` +
             `${r.status}. ${r.ok
               ? 'It renders, so this column adds no join.'
               : `It does not, so this column IS a join: ${r.text}`}`);
    }

    // ---- Does a lookup's ADDITIONAL FIELD projection cost its own join? --
    // The "Add a column to show each of these additional fields" option makes
    // a DEPENDENT lookup: a second Lookup field pointing at the same target,
    // tied to the primary through the Field element's FieldRef attribute,
    // projecting a different column of that target.
    //
    // Whether the pair costs one join or two decides whether a check can count
    // declared ref columns, or has to count projections. Two lookups that read
    // the same foreign row COULD share a join; that they do not is not
    // obvious either way, which is why it is measured.
    //
    // Shaped so the answer is binary: 11 lookups + the primary is 12 and must
    // render; adding only its dependent then tips to 13 if the projection is
    // its own join, and stays at 12 if it rides on the primary.
    const primary = await readField('Parent');
    const primaryId = (!readFailed(primary) && primary.body.Id) || '';
    if (PROBE_JOINS && primaryId && readFailed(await readField('ParentStatus'))) {
      const made = await addField(
        `<Field Type="Lookup" DisplayName="ParentStatus" Name="ParentStatus" `
        + `List="{${parent.Id}}" ShowField="PickStatus" FieldRef="${primaryId}"/>`);
      log(made.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
          `dependent lookup ParentStatus ${made.ok ? 'created' : `FAILED HTTP ${made.status} ${made.text.slice(0, 220)}`}`);
    }
    const dependent = !readFailed(await readField('ParentStatus'));
    if (!worked || worked < 2 || !dependent) {
      record('JOINPRJ', "Does a lookup's additional-field projection cost its own join?",
             'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] ` + (dependent
               ? 'no ceiling to work from.'
               : 'ParentStatus does not exist. Set PROBE_JOINS and re-paste; it '
                 + 'is created as a dependent lookup via the FieldRef attribute.'));
    } else {
      const base = available.slice(0, worked - 1);
      const withPrimary = await tryProject(['Title', ...base, 'Parent']);
      const withBoth = await tryProject(['Title', ...base, 'Parent', 'ParentStatus']);
      // A pass only means "free" if the dependent was actually projected. If
      // it is absent from the returned row it was dropped, and the view
      // rendered because it was never asked to do the extra work.
      const projected = withBoth.ok && withBoth.present('ParentStatus');
      record('JOINPRJ', "Does a lookup's additional-field projection cost its own join?",
             !withPrimary.ok ? 'NOT ESTABLISHED (the control did not render)'
               : !withBoth.ok ? 'ITS OWN JOIN'
               : projected ? 'FREE (it rides on its primary)'
               : 'NOT ESTABLISHED (rendered, but the dependent was NOT returned)',
             `[${stamp}] ${base.length} lookup(s) + Parent, HTTP ` +
             `${withPrimary.status}; the same plus its dependent ParentStatus, ` +
             `HTTP ${withBoth.status}, ParentStatus ` +
             `${withBoth.ok ? (projected ? 'PRESENT' : 'ABSENT') : 'n/a'} in the ` +
             `returned row (${withBoth.keys} key(s)). ` + (
               !withPrimary.ok
                 ? `The control failed, so neither reading is usable: ${withPrimary.text}`
                 : !withBoth.ok
                   ? `Adding only the projection breaks it, so each costs its own `
                     + `join and a check must count PROJECTIONS: ${withBoth.text}`
                   : projected
                     ? 'Both render AND the dependent came back, so two '
                       + 'projections of one target cost one join: a check counts '
                       + 'ref COLUMNS, not projections.'
                     : 'The view rendered without returning the dependent, so it '
                       + 'was dropped rather than served free. Says nothing about '
                       + 'join cost.'
             ));
    }
  }

  // ---- Does the ORDER of an AND's conditions decide anything? ----------
  // analysis/checks/_views.py ships this hedge: "An index is necessary but may
  // not be sufficient, because SharePoint also considers filter order,
  // selectivity and condition shape." Filter order is the testable third of it.
  //
  // Shadow carries byte-identical data to Bucket, so `Bucket='Z' AND
  // Shadow='Z'` matches exactly the rows either matches alone, the same 60 as
  // CMPIDX and CMPUNI. The AND restricts nothing. So this pair differs from
  // those two, and from each other, in ONE respect: which condition comes
  // first, and therefore which column SharePoint reaches for an index on.
  //
  //   both SERVED        -> order does not decide it; an indexed condition
  //                         anywhere in the AND is enough, and the hedge can
  //                         name selectivity and shape only.
  //   ANDIDX only        -> order decides it. A view whose FIRST filter column
  //                         is unindexed is broken however well-indexed the
  //                         rest is, and that is a rule this project can check.
  //   both REFUSED       -> an AND touching an unindexed column breaches
  //                         whatever the order; an index helps a simple filter
  //                         and not a compound one.
  //   ANDUNI only        -> incoherent against CMPIDX. Re-run before recording.
  const AND_IDX_FIRST =
    `<Eq><FieldRef Name='Bucket'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq>`;
  const AND_UNI_FIRST =
    `<Eq><FieldRef Name='Shadow'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq>`;
  await askCaml(
    'ANDIDX', 'CAML And, INDEXED condition FIRST',
    `<Where><And>${AND_IDX_FIRST}${AND_UNI_FIRST}</And></Where>`, matched);
  await askCaml(
    'ANDUNI', 'CAML And, UNINDEXED condition FIRST (same rows, reversed)',
    `<Where><And>${AND_UNI_FIRST}${AND_IDX_FIRST}</And></Where>`, matched);

  // ---- Can CAML do what OData refuses on a Calculated column? ---------
  // OData rejects a Calculated column in BOTH a sort and a filter expression,
  // by type and at any list size: "The field 'PickCond' of type 'Calculated'
  // cannot be used in the query filter expression."
  //
  // That must NOT be read as "views cannot filter on a calculated column". A
  // view renders CAML, OData is a different code path, and this fixture has
  // already been wrong once by carrying an OData result over to a CAML
  // question. The SharePoint UI does offer calculated columns as view filters,
  // so the two plausibly disagree, and if they do, it is the CAML answer that
  // governs analysis/checks/_views.py.
  //
  // CalcBait is `=CONCATENATE("c-",Title)` and Titles are unique, so this
  // matches exactly one row of 6,000 (selective, and past the threshold on a
  // column that CANNOT carry an index, since CALCIDX establishes calculated
  // columns are not indexable). A SERVED here is therefore also a second
  // reading on whether selectivity alone can carry a query.
  await askCaml(
    'CALCAM', 'CAML Eq on a CALCULATED column (OData refuses this by type)',
    "<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='CalcBait'/>"
    + "<Value Type='Text'>c-Row 000003</Value></Eq></Where>", 1);

  // ---- The lookup rule, on the path the check actually governs ---------
  // _views.py warns that an indexed Person or Lookup column does not avert the
  // threshold, on Microsoft's documentation. OData contradicted it. These ask
  // the same question in CAML, which is what a VIEW renders, and in both
  // forms, because filtering a lookup by its stored id and filtering by its
  // projected value are different amounts of work and the documented rule may
  // only be about the second.
  if (ownerId) {
    await askCaml(
      'PERCAM', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, LookupId form',
      `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Owner' LookupId='TRUE'/>` +
      `<Value Type='Integer'>${ownerId}</Value></Eq></Where>`, matched);
    // The display name for OWNER_ID, read rather than assumed. It is NOT
    // necessarily the current user, and a wrong name here matches nothing,
    // returns cleanly and would read as a finding about the threshold.
    const owner = await spGet(`web/getuserbyid(${ownerId})?$select=Id,Title`);
    if (!readFailed(owner) && owner.body.Title) {
      await askCaml(
        'PERCAV', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, projected-value form',
        `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Owner'/>` +
        `<Value Type='Text'>${owner.body.Title}</Value></Eq></Where>`, matched);
    } else {
      record('PERCAV', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, projected-value form',
             'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] could not read the display name for user ${ownerId} ` +
             `(HTTP ${owner.status}), so no projected-value filter was sent.`);
    }
  } else {
    for (const [id, question] of [
      ['PERCAM', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, LookupId form'],
      ['PERCAV', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, projected-value form'],
    ]) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             'No owner id: OWNER_ID is 0 and none could be discovered.');
    }
  }
  await askCaml(
    'LOOCAM', `CAML Eq on INDEXED Lookup, LookupId form (target holds ${parentSize})`,
    `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Parent' LookupId='TRUE'/>` +
    `<Value Type='Integer'>${parentItemId}</Value></Eq></Where>`, matched);

  // ---- The rule as a VIEW actually exercises it -----------------------
  // Same selective filter as LOOCAM and PERCAM, but the lookup column is now in
  // ViewFields, so the query must JOIN to the target list for every row it
  // returns. That is what Microsoft's indexed-lookup rule is about, and what
  // adding `Parent` to an All Items view of this fixture reproduces: "The
  // number of items in the list exceeds the list view threshold, which is 5000
  // items."
  //
  // SERVED here contradicts the rule on the path _views.py governs. REFUSED
  // confirms it, and confirms that the join rather than the filter is what the
  // threshold charges for, which is also the difference between these two
  // rows and LOOCAM/PERCAM above.
  await askCaml(
    'LOOPRJ',
    `CAML Eq on INDEXED Lookup, PROJECTING it into a ${parentSize}-item target`,
    `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Parent' LookupId='TRUE'/>` +
    `<Value Type='Integer'>${parentItemId}</Value></Eq></Where>`,
    matched, ['Title', 'Parent']);
  if (ownerId) {
    await askCaml(
      'PERPRJ', 'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, PROJECTING the person (the real view shape)',
      `<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name='Owner' LookupId='TRUE'/>` +
      `<Value Type='Integer'>${ownerId}</Value></Eq></Where>`,
      matched, ['Title', 'Owner']);
  } else {
    record('PERPRJ',
           'CAML Eq on INDEXED Person, PROJECTING the person (the real view shape)',
           'NOT ESTABLISHED',
           'No owner id: OWNER_ID is 0 and none could be discovered.');
  }

  // ---- Is an index NECESSARY for a presence test, or only sufficient? --
  // NULCAM establishes that an INDEXED column's presence test is served. It
  // cannot say whether the index is what does it, because every DateTime column
  // in the original fixture is indexed. This pair closes that: same data, same
  // 60 rows, same operator, differing ONLY in the index.
  //
  //   NNIDX served + NNUNI refused  -> the index is NECESSARY. The remedy in
  //                                    _views.py is not merely safe, it is the
  //                                    thing that makes the view work.
  //   both served                   -> a presence test does not need an index
  //                                    at this selectivity, and the remedy is
  //                                    sufficient but not required.
  //   NNIDX refused                 -> incoherent against NULCAM. Re-run before
  //                                    recording anything.
  if (!seedOk) {
    for (const [id, question] of [
      ['NNIDX', 'CAML IsNotNull, INDEXED DateTime'],
      ['NNUNI', 'CAML IsNotNull, UNINDEXED DateTime (is an index NECESSARY?)'],
    ]) {
      record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
             `[${stamp}] the is-not-null pair is not seeded to ${matched} ` +
             'row(s), so neither column has a known population.');
    }
  } else {
    await askCaml(
      'NNIDX', 'CAML IsNotNull, INDEXED DateTime',
      "<Where><IsNotNull><FieldRef Name='NotNullIdx'/></IsNotNull></Where>",
      matched);
    await askCaml(
      'NNUNI', 'CAML IsNotNull, UNINDEXED DateTime (is an index NECESSARY?)',
      "<Where><IsNotNull><FieldRef Name='NotNullUni'/></IsNotNull></Where>",
      matched);
  }

  // NOT selectivity-matched, and unfixably so: the loader owns every row, so
  // Author and Editor are the same principal throughout and Created/Modified
  // span one load. Each therefore matches the WHOLE list, which breaches on
  // result-set size alone. Recorded with expectedRows=null and the caveat in
  // the evidence, because a REFUSED here says nothing about indexing while a
  // SERVED would be genuinely surprising.
  const SYS_CAVEAT =
    ' NOTE: this filter matches every row in the list, so a threshold refusal ' +
    'is attributable to result-set size and is NOT evidence about indexing. ' +
    'Only a SERVED is informative here.';
  for (const [id, question, filter] of [
    ['SYSCRE', 'OData comparison on Created', "Created ge datetime'2020-01-01T00:00:00Z'"],
    ['SYSMOD', 'OData comparison on Modified', "Modified ge datetime'2020-01-01T00:00:00Z'"],
    ['SYSAUT', 'OData comparison on Author', `AuthorId ne 0`],
    ['SYSEDI', 'OData comparison on Editor', `EditorId ne 0`],
  ]) {
    const r = await spGet(
      `${items}?$select=Id&$top=${PAGE}&$filter=${encodeURIComponent(filter)}`);
    record(id, question, judge(r, null),
           `[${stamp}] $filter=${filter}, HTTP ${r.status}: ` +
           `${r.body ? JSON.stringify(r.body).slice(0, 200) : '(no JSON body)'}${SYS_CAVEAT}`);
  }
  };

  // Outside `measureFilters`, and called unconditionally, because this is
  // the one question here that does not need a declared row count. Run of
  // 2026-08-17: it sat inside and reported four rows unreached on a fixture
  // holding 5,614 of the 6,000 loaded, which is exactly the case it was
  // rewritten to answer.
  const measureGuard = async () => {
    // ---- Does the #272 guard change what is SERVED past the threshold? ---
    // Deliberately OUTSIDE the checkpoint gate above, because this question
    // does not need a declared row count. It needs the list to be past the
    // threshold, and it compares the guarded query against the UNGUARDED one
    // measured in the same run, on the same list, seconds apart.
    //
    // That is what makes it readable on a fixture that has drifted. The run of
    // 2026-08-17 found 5,614 rows where 6,000 were loaded, and an absolute
    // expected count would have been unreadable; a comparison is not, because
    // both halves see whatever the list now holds. Every other row here asks
    // "how many", so they keep the stricter gate.
    const guardBlocked =
      count < 0 ? 'ItemCount could not be read'
      : count <= LIST_VIEW_THRESHOLD
        ? `ItemCount ${count} is not past the ${LIST_VIEW_THRESHOLD} threshold, `
          + 'so nothing here is throttled and the comparison says nothing'
      : '';

    // One line, unbroken, so a test can compare it to the emitted constant.
    const GUARD = '<Or><IsNotNull><FieldRef Name="ID"/></IsNotNull><IsNull><FieldRef Name="ID"/></IsNull></Or>';
    const guarded = (where) => `<Where><And>${where}${GUARD}</And></Where>`;
    const IDX_LEAF = `<Eq><FieldRef Name='Bucket'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq>`;
    const UNI_LEAF = `<Eq><FieldRef Name='Shadow'/><Value Type='Text'>${RARE_BUCKET}</Value></Eq>`;
    const NUL_LEAF = "<IsNull><FieldRef Name='ClosedAt'/></IsNull>";

    // Rows and status, judged by nothing. The caller compares.
    const rawCaml = async (where) => {
      const digest = await getDigest();
      const viewXml = `<View><Query>${where}</Query><RowLimit>${PAGE}</RowLimit></View>`;
      const r = await spPost(
        `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(LIST)}')/RenderListDataAsStream`,
        { parameters: { ViewXml: viewXml } }, digest);
      const rows = (r.ok && r.body && Array.isArray(r.body.Row)) ? r.body.Row.length : null;
      return { ok: r.ok, status: r.status, rows, text: (r.text || '').slice(0, 200) };
    };

    const guardPair = async (id, question, leaf, expectServed) => {
      if (guardBlocked) {
        record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED', `[${stamp}] not measured: ${guardBlocked}.`);
        return;
      }
      const bare = await rawCaml(`<Where>${leaf}</Where>`);
      const wrapped = await rawCaml(guarded(leaf));
      const same = bare.ok === wrapped.ok && bare.rows === wrapped.rows;
      // The unguarded half is the reference, so a run where IT disagrees with
      // what this row expects says nothing about the guard.
      if (bare.ok !== expectServed) {
        record(id, question, 'NOT ESTABLISHED',
          `[${stamp}] the UNGUARDED twin was ${bare.ok ? 'served' : 'refused'} `
          + `(HTTP ${bare.status}) where this row expects it to be `
          + `${expectServed ? 'served' : 'refused'}, so there is no reference to `
          + `compare the guarded form against. ${bare.text}`);
        return;
      }
      record(id, question,
        same ? (bare.ok ? 'UNCHANGED (both served)' : 'UNCHANGED (both refused)')
             : 'CHANGED',
        `[${stamp}] unguarded: HTTP ${bare.status}, ${bare.rows} row(s). guarded: `
        + `HTTP ${wrapped.status}, ${wrapped.rows} row(s). `
        + (same
          ? 'The conjunct changed neither the outcome nor the row count on a list '
            + `of ${count}, past the ${LIST_VIEW_THRESHOLD} threshold.`
          : 'THE CONJUNCT CHANGED WHAT SHAREPOINT SERVED. #272 emits it on every '
            + 'view, so a filter that worked at this size stops working, on the '
            + `largest lists, and no build can see it. ${wrapped.text}`));
    };

    // The two measurements. Both twins are on INDEXED columns and are expected
    // to be served, so a change here is the guard's doing.
    await guardPair('GRDIDX', 'CAML comparison, INDEXED Text, GUARDED as #272 emits it',
                    IDX_LEAF, true);
    await guardPair('GRDNUL', 'CAML IsNull, INDEXED DateTime, GUARDED as #272 emits it',
                    NUL_LEAF, true);
    // The control: the UNINDEXED twin, which must be refused at this size. If
    // guarding it makes it served, the conjunct changed plan selection and
    // GRDIDX being green says nothing about the index. Requires IDXCLR to have
    // restored the control, and IDXSET says whether it did.
    await guardPair('GRDUNI', 'CAML comparison, UNINDEXED twin, GUARDED (control)',
                    UNI_LEAF, false);

    // The guard alone, matching every row in the list, so it must be refused
    // for the same reason any unfiltered query is. Served would mean SharePoint
    // treats it as satisfiable without scanning, which would make it a filter
    // rather than the inert conjunct every other row assumes.
    if (guardBlocked) {
      record('GRDONLY', 'CAML the guard ALONE, matching every row (control)',
             'NOT ESTABLISHED', `[${stamp}] not measured: ${guardBlocked}.`);
    } else {
      const alone = await rawCaml(`<Where>${GUARD}</Where>`);
      record('GRDONLY', 'CAML the guard ALONE, matching every row (control)',
             alone.ok ? 'SERVED' : 'REFUSED',
             `[${stamp}] HTTP ${alone.status}, ${alone.rows} row(s) of ${count}. `
             + (alone.ok
               ? 'SERVED is the surprise: the guard matches every row, so a query '
                 + 'with nothing else in it should breach the threshold. Read '
                 + 'GRDIDX with that in mind. ' + alone.text
               : 'Refused, as an unfiltered query at this size should be, so the '
                 + 'guard is not being treated as a selective filter.'));
    }
  };

  if (!blocked) await measureFilters();
  await measureGuard();

  report();
  // report() counts only literal NOT ESTABLISHED outcomes, so a table that is
  // void for any other reason reads as reassuring. Say so after the summary,
  // which is the last thing on screen and the thing that gets pasted back.
  if (blocked) {
    log('FAIL', `*** Nothing above is evidence: ${blocked}.`);
  } else if (wrong.length) {
    log('FAIL', '*** The index flags are not what they claim. See IDXSET.');
  }

  // ---- Cleanup --------------------------------------------------------
  if (!CLEANUP_AT_END) {
    log('INFO', `Fixture lists remain: '${LIST}' and '${PARENT}'.`);
    log('INFO', 'When finished, set CLEANUP_AT_END=true with both write gates');
    log('INFO', 'and re-paste. Expect more than one pass past 5,000 rows.');
    return;
  }

  // Past 5,000 items a list will not empty in one page, and a cleanup that
  // silently leaves rows behind lets THIS run's rows answer the NEXT run's
  // questions. So: page until empty or until a page stops making progress,
  // then say exactly what is left rather than reporting success.
  const emptyList = async (title) => {
    let removed = 0;
    let throttled = 0;
    for (let page = 0; page < 20; page += 1) {
      const found = await spGet(
        `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')/items?$select=Id&$top=1000`);
      const rows = (found.ok && found.body && found.body.value) || [];
      if (!rows.length) break;
      // One digest per PAGE, not per item. A form digest lives about thirty
      // minutes, so refreshing it 6,000 times doubled the request count for
      // nothing, and request count is the binding constraint here.
      const digest = await getDigest();
      let removedThisPage = 0;
      for (const row of rows) {
        const gone = await spPost(
          `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')/items(${row.Id})/recycle`, {}, digest);
        if (gone.ok) removedThisPage += 1;
        else if (gone.status === 429) throttled += 1;
      }
      removed += removedThisPage;
      // A page that removed nothing will not do better on the next attempt,
      // and looping twenty times over it would bury the reason.
      if (removedThisPage === 0) {
        log('FAIL',
            `CLEANUP '${title}': a page of ${rows.length} row(s) would not recycle ` +
            `(${throttled} were HTTP 429). Throttling is the likeliest cause at ` +
            `this size; retention, a lock or permissions are the others. Stopping.`);
        break;
      }
    }
    const left = await spGet(`web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')?$select=ItemCount`);
    const remaining = (left.ok && left.body) ? left.body.ItemCount : -1;
    log(remaining === 0 ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        `CLEANUP '${title}': recycled ${removed} item(s), ${remaining} REMAINING` +
        (remaining === 0 ? '.' : '. Re-run with CLEANUP_AT_END until this reads 0.'));
    return remaining === 0;
  };

  // CHILD FIRST. SharePoint refuses to recycle a list that a live Lookup
  // column still targets, so the parent cannot go until the child has. This is
  // the reverse of provisioning order, and calculated-operand-probe.js already
  // learned it the hard way.
  for (const title of [LIST, PARENT]) {
    await emptyList(title);
    const digest = await getDigest();
    const recycled = await spPost(
      `web/lists/getbytitle('${odata(title)}')/recycle`, {}, digest);
    log(recycled.ok ? 'OK' : 'FAIL',
        recycled.ok
          ? `Recycled '${title}'. Restorable from the site recycle bin.`
          : `Could not recycle '${title}': HTTP ${recycled.status} ${recycled.text.slice(0, 300)}`);
  }
})();
